# pond/agent/conversation — friction log

Gaps in the language/stdlib substrate, deviations from
`pond/CONTRACTS.md`, and suspicions that surfaced while
building `pond/agent/conversation/`.

---

## Suspected duplicate: tab-separated kv stream walker

This is the THIRD lib in pond/ that encodes its public surface
as a tab-separated stream (`role\tcontent\trole\tcontent`):

- `pond/router::RouteParams.path_kv` — `"k1\tv1\tk2\tv2"`
- `pond/agent/llm::LlmRequest.messages` — `"role\tcontent..."`
- `pond/agent/conversation::Conversation.history_buf` — same shape

`pond/agent/llm/wire.hl` already factored its own `__next_field`
+ `__Slice` walker; `pond/router` has its own; this lib has
`drop_front_messages` which walks the bytes once to count tab
boundaries.

**duplicate-suspected** — a stdlib `std::iter::TabFields { value:
String; next: Int; done: Bool; }` with companion
`std::iter::tab_first(s) -> TabFields` /
`std::iter::tab_next(prev) -> TabFields` would let every
consumer drop its bespoke walker. The tab-as-delimiter shape
isn't going away (it's the v1 substitute for parametric
`Vec<(K,V)>`), so the walker is too.

Also flagged: the implicit "callers don't embed tabs in field
values" contract that every tab-stream consumer inherits. A
stdlib walker pair could add an escape convention (`\t` →
literal tab, `\\` → backslash) and centralize it.

---

## Substrate gap: per-conversation topic subjects share one wire — [BLOCKED ON CONTRACT DELTA]

**2026-06-12 retest (hale HEAD b716696).** The routing-key
machinery works end-to-end: `topic T { payload: P; keyed_by f; }`
+ `subscribe T as h where key == EXPR;` builds and filters
delivery correctly (verified by /tmp probe — a publisher firing
keys 1 and 2 reaches only the `where key == 1` subscriber).
BUT routing-key fields must be **int-shaped**:

```
type error: topic `Updated`'s `keyed_by` field `conv_id` has
type `String`; routing-key fields must be int-shaped (Int,
Decimal, Time, Duration, Bool, or a no-payload enum)
```

So closing this entry is NOT mechanical: `Message` carries no
conversation id at all (role/content/ts per CONTRACTS.md), and
`Conversation.params` has no id either. The fix needs a contract
delta — add `conversation_id: Int` to the `ConversationUpdated`
payload (either on `Message` or via a dedicated
`ConversationUpdate { conversation_id: Int; message: Message; }`
payload type) plus an `id: Int` param on `Conversation` — then
`keyed_by conversation_id` lands in one line. Deferred to the
next CONTRACTS.md amendment pass.

**Current source shape (still in place).** `ConversationUpdated`
is a single topic decl; two distinct `Conversation` instances
publishing on it share a single wire subject. Every subscriber
sees every conversation's traffic. Workaround remains "bind
owner+consumer 1:1".

---

## 2026-06-12 — G1 literal-subject workaround retired — [CLOSED]

WS3.3 (upstream post-audit 2026-06-11) fixed cross-file /
cross-seed topic resolution on the publish side, so the lib now
ships the CONTRACTS.md-verbatim form: `bus { publish
ConversationUpdated; }` + `ConversationUpdated <- m;` in
`conversation.hl`, resolving the `topic ConversationUpdated`
decl. The old literal-subject workaround (`publish
"conv.updated" of type Message;` + `"conv.updated" <- m;`) is
gone. (The decl is CO-LOCATED with the publisher rather than in
a sibling `topics.hl`: `hale check` still resolves topics
file-locally even though build/run accept sibling decls since
WS3.3 — co-location keeps the `hale check` gate green. Restore
the sibling layout when the upstream check-vs-build divergence
closes.)

Verified 2026-06-12: lib typechecks; `examples/two-turn` builds
+ runs; a cross-seed consumer probe with
`subscribe conv::ConversationUpdated as on_update;` receives
both appended messages.

**Breaking note for out-of-tree subscribers:** the wire subject
is no longer the literal `"conv.updated"` — subscribers must
bind the topic decl (`conv::ConversationUpdated`). No in-repo
consumer used the literal subject.


