# pond/agent/llm — FRICTION

Gaps, suspicions, and deviations from CONTRACTS.md surfaced while
building this lib.

## two-channel-rule: locus methods can't declare fallible(E) — [CLOSED 2026-06-08]

**Closed.** v0.8.1 narrowed the two-channel rule (#24 v0.2,
commits `d565d6f` + `98910b9`): user-declared `fn` member fns
now carry `fallible(E)`. The source pass landed —
`AnthropicClient.complete` / `OpenAiClient.complete` are now
`-> LlmResponse fallible(LlmError)` methods that propagate with
`or raise`. The old non-fallible-method workaround is gone:
the `__record` / `__sse_recover` error-check fns and the
`last_error_kind/status/detail` accessor triple (plus their
`last_*` params) are deleted. The fallible free fns
(`anthropic_complete` / `openai_complete`) stay as the kernel
the methods wrap and as a public value-channel entry point.

## bus-literal-subjects: two publisher files use literal-subject publishes — [RE-SCOPED 2026-06-12: idiom, not workaround]

**Updated 2026-06-12.** Upstream WS3.3 (2026-06-11, in HEAD
`43300e5`) fixed cross-file bus topic resolution: `bus
{ publish T; }` / `T <- v;` now resolve a `topic T` declared in
a sibling `.hl` file, under both `hale build` and `hale run`.
The "topic decls are file-local to the publisher" behavior this
entry originally recorded was a `hale run <dir>` import bug, not
a codegen rule. Verified by probe at HEAD: a 3-file seed with
`topic Ping` in one file, an ident-form publisher in a sibling
file, and a subscriber builds and delivers the payload.

This lib nonetheless KEEPS the literal-subject form — it has TWO
publisher files (`anthropic.hl` and `openai.hl`), both publishing
the chunk/done channels, and the literal-subject form (`publish
"agent.llm.chunk" of type LlmChunkMsg;` + `"agent.llm.chunk" <-
LlmChunkMsg { ... };`) remains the right idiom for topics with
>= 2 publisher files. So what was a forced workaround is now a
deliberate idiom choice. The literal subjects stay byte-identical
to the `subject:` fields of the `topic LlmChunk` / `topic
LlmDone` decls in `topics.hl` (renamed from `wire_topics.hl`,
see codegen-file-order below), so cross-seed subscribers
(`pond/agent/conversation`) keep wiring `subscribe llm::LlmChunk`
unchanged. The wire subjects `"agent.llm.chunk"` /
`"agent.llm.done"` are a compatibility contract — do not rename.

## topic-vs-type-duality: bus payload can't be raw String

`topic LlmChunk { payload: String; }` is the CONTRACTS.md
shape. The codegen rejects raw String at a bus send site:

```
codegen error: unsupported in codegen v0: bus send payload
  must be a user-type or has-payload enum value; got String
```

This is the v1 bus-send contract — every payload must be a
user-defined type so the bus can lay it out as a known record
(field-by-field copy). Wrap the String in a single-field
wrapper and the send becomes `"agent.llm.chunk" <-
LlmChunkMsg { payload: delta };`. We do that — `topics.hl`
declares the thin `LlmChunkMsg` / `LlmDoneMsg` wrapper types
alongside the `topic LlmChunk` / `topic LlmDone` decls (the
topics carry the wrappers as their payload type, and exist so
cross-seed subscribers get the ident-form `subscribe
llm::LlmChunk` surface).

## codegen-file-order: type forward-refs fail in topic-decl files — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

**Closed.** Cross-file resolution is order-free at upstream HEAD
(`43300e5`, post-audit 2026-06-11). The `wire_topics.hl`
name-around-the-sort-order trick is retired: the file is back to
its natural name `topics.hl`, which sorts BEFORE `types.hl` and
still builds clean — the `payload: LlmResponse` forward-ref into
`types.hl` resolves. Verified 2026-06-12 with `hale build
agent/llm/` plus the echo-completion example build + run.

Historical record: files in a seed used to be processed in
alphabetical order at codegen, so `type LlmDoneMsg { payload:
LlmResponse; }` in a `topics.hl` that sorted before `types.hl`
failed with `unknown type name __lib_llm_types_LlmResponse in
signature`. Worked around 2026-05-24 by naming the file
`wire_topics.hl` so it sorted after `types.hl` and `wire.hl`.

## eager-buffering: streaming path drains the whole response first

`AnthropicClient.stream` and `OpenAiClient.stream` send the
request with `stream: true` in the body + `Accept:
text/event-stream`, but the implementation drains the entire
HTTP response off the socket via `__drain_stream` BEFORE
walking the SSE frames and firing per-chunk `LlmChunk` topics.

A true low-latency client would feed each `recv_bytes` chunk
into the SSE buffer and publish chunks as they arrive:

```hale
// Aspirational shape:
while !stream_done {
    let chunk = s.recv_bytes(8192);
    if len(chunk) == 0 { break; }
    sse_buf = sse::feed_chunk(sse_buf, chunk);
    while {
        let line = sse::next_data_line(sse_buf);
        if !line.has_line { break; }
        sse_buf = line.remaining;
        // publish delta + accumulate text
    }
}
```

The v1 shape sacrifices that for a simpler control flow (one
round-trip drain, then one pass over the body) because:

- HTTP/1.1 with `Connection: close` means the server closes
  the socket after the response, so draining to EOF gives us
  the whole response cleanly.
- Interleaving send + recv on the same fd with mid-recv
  publish doesn't bend naturally to the current
  `std::io::tcp::Stream` surface; we'd need a `Stream` mode
  that yields between recv calls so the bus can drain its
  mailbox.
- Short prompts (the typical agent-orchestration case) fit
  in one or two recv chunks anyway — the difference is
  invisible.

**Future**: a chunked-recv mode + an `on_chunk: fn(Stream)
-> Bool` callback on `__drain_stream` would let the streaming
loop publish per-chunk without changing the public surface.

## duplicate-suspected: SSE framing

The SSE-frame buffer + `data:` extractor in `sse.hl` is
duplicate-suspected. The Anthropic streaming format, the
OpenAI streaming format, and (as workloads materialize) any
other LLM vendor's streaming format all use the same
`\n\n`-separated frame shape with `data:` payload prefix.
A second pond consumer + this lib + the hypothetical third
LLM vendor = 3 hits, the moment for a lift into `pond/sse/`
as a dedicated lib.

Held off because:
- Only one current consumer (this lib).
- `sse::next_data_line` carries vendor-specific end-of-stream
  sentinels (`[DONE]` for OpenAI, `event: message_stop` for
  Anthropic). Lifting needs a hook for per-vendor sentinels.
- The whole module is 100 lines — cheap to copy until the
  third consumer.

## duplicate-suspected: JSON request shaping

`wire.hl`'s `__build_anthropic_body` and `__build_openai_body`
share a tab-separated message walker (`__next_field`) plus
the boilerplate `b.begin_object()` / `b.string_field()` etc.
Two adapters today; a third vendor (Gemini, Mistral, etc.)
would push the shared pieces up.

The walker (`__next_field` over tab-separated strings) is the
broader duplicate — `pond/router` does the same over its
`RouteParams.path_kv`, `pond/agent/conversation` does it over
history. A `std::iter::TabFields` (or `std::str::split_tab`)
would lift the pattern. Stays inlined here.

## duplicate-suspected: __extract_field_raw is a copy of stdlib internal — [CLOSED 2026-08-03: stdlib surface shipped, helper deleted]

**Closed:** upstream delivered the fix path better than asked —
`std::json::find_field_raw(json, name) -> String` is PUBLIC
(rebuilt on the object cursor in v0.11.18, spec'd in
spec/stdlib.md § std::json with the re-feed-the-substring
chaining contract this lib's nested walks already use). All 10
call sites (wire.hl ×5 incl. __extract_error_detail, sse.hl ×5)
now call it directly; `__extract_field_raw` AND `__key_pos`
(~144 lines) are deleted. Gates: check ok, test 2/2 (wire_test
covers content/usage/message extraction, sse_test the delta
paths), echo-completion builds. The typed
`find_object_field`/`find_array_field` split asked for below
never happened — the untyped raw surface is strictly more
general and did the job.

`__extract_field_raw` in `wire.hl` does the same job as
`std::json`'s internal `__json_find_field_raw` — find a
top-level field and return its raw token (preserving brace /
bracket / quote nesting). We need this for the *nested*
object case (Anthropic's `usage`, OpenAI's `choices[0].
message`) that the flat `find_string_field` / `find_int_field`
helpers don't descend into. The stdlib helper isn't exported.

**Fix path** (in stdlib): export
`std::json::find_object_field(json, name) -> String` and
`std::json::find_array_field(json, name) -> String` so the
nested case has a typed surface. Same code, exported name.

## design-question: LlmRequest.messages encoding

CONTRACTS.md declares `LlmRequest.messages: String` with no
specified encoding. This lib chose tab-separated
"role\tcontent\trole\tcontent\t..." pairs because:

- Mirrors `pond/router::RouteParams.path_kv` ("k1\tv1\tk2\tv2")
  and `pond/agent/conversation::Conversation.history` (per
  CONTRACTS.md "tab-separated messages").
- Keeps `LlmRequest` constructable from a struct literal with
  no per-message allocation.
- Round-trips through `__next_field` cleanly at the wire-
  format-build step.

Alternatives considered:

1. **JSON string** — `messages: String` carries `[{"role":...},
   ...]` ready for inline-quote into the wire body. Forces
   callers to escape strings themselves and re-parses our own
   output. Heavier for the common case.
2. **Newline-separated** — same shape with `\n` instead of
   `\t`. Conflicts with the v1 `read_line` family.
3. **Repeat fields** — `LlmRequest { messages_0_role: "user",
   messages_0_content: "...", ... }`. Hard cap on count;
   ugly.

Sticking with tab-separated for v1; the shape is documented
in the README + in `LlmRequest`'s field comment.

## design-question: Anthropic's mandatory max_tokens

The Anthropic API REQUIRES `max_tokens` in the request body
(non-optional). CONTRACTS.md's `LlmRequest.max_tokens` defaults
to `0` (meaning "use vendor default"). We resolve by inserting
a fallback of 1024 when `max_tokens <= 0` at the wire-build
step (`__build_anthropic_body`):

```hale
let mut max_tok = req.max_tokens;
if max_tok <= 0 { max_tok = 1024; }
b.int_field("max_tokens", max_tok);
```

OpenAI's `max_tokens` IS optional — we omit the field when
`<= 0`. The asymmetry surfaces a CONTRACTS.md design choice:
either bump the default to 1024 (or some other reasonable
value) at the type-decl site, or document the per-vendor
behavior (current state).

## design-question: streaming surface returns Unit

`fn stream(req: LlmRequest);` returns Unit per CONTRACTS.md —
the implication is that all output flows through the bus
(`LlmChunk` + `LlmDone`). We honor that. Two open questions:

1. **Synchronous or async?** Today the method blocks on the
   recv-drain loop, then publishes everything inline. A
   future "real" streaming impl (see "eager-buffering" above)
   would still be synchronous-from-the-caller's-perspective
   — the bus is the async piece.
2. **Where do errors surface?** `stream()` returns Unit, so it
   has no value channel — on a fetch failure it emits a
   sentinel `LlmDone` (empty `LlmResponse` payload) so
   subscribers learn the stream finished. Subscribers can't
   distinguish "success with empty response" from "failed
   call" on the bus today (the `complete()` path is the one
   with hard `fallible(LlmError)` semantics). A future
   `LlmFailed` topic carrying the LlmError would let
   subscribers tell them apart on the bus directly.


## bug found by unit tests: flat-finder first-match shadows the "text" key — FIXED in-lib 2026-07-04

`__parse_anthropic_response` returned empty `text` for every
REALISTIC non-streaming response: a content block is
`{"type":"text","text":"..."}`, and
`std::json::find_string_field(element, "text")` first matches
the needle `"text"` inside the VALUE of `"type"`, sees no colon,
and returns "" — it never retries later occurrences. The SSE
streaming path dodged it only because there the type value is
`text_delta` (which the quoted needle doesn't match), so the
demo never caught it. Found by `tests/wire_test.hl`.

In-lib fix: new `__key_pos(json, name)` — absolute index of the
QUOTED needle followed (after optional ws) by a colon, retrying
past value matches — now gates both the content-block text
lookup (stdlib finder runs on the slice from the key) and
`__extract_field_raw` (which previously returned "" on the same
collision instead of retrying). Regression-guarded by
wire_test.hl asserting the real vendor shape.

Upstream ask (extends the `__extract_field_raw`
duplicate-suspected entry above): std::json's flat finders
should retry-scan until a colon follows the key match — any
JSON document where a searched key's name appears as an earlier
string VALUE hits this class.

**2026-08-03 follow-up — upstream ask DELIVERED (hale v0.11.18),
in-lib workaround retired at the content-block site.** The
stdlib finders were rebuilt on a single-pass object cursor
("matches key positions, not key text", Crumb 4-2): probe
confirmed `find_string_field({"type":"text","text":"Hel"},
"text")` now returns `Hel`. The same rebuild ENFORCES the
whole-value chaining contract — feeding a mid-document tail
slice (this lib's old workaround shape) now returns "" instead
of scanning forward, which is exactly how wire_test.hl caught
the change (masked until 2026-08-03 behind the Stream-send
codegen failure below). `__parse_anthropic_response` now calls
the stdlib finder on the intact array element. (*Same-day
correction:* the raw-field helper turned out to be exported —
`std::json::find_field_raw` is public — so the
duplicate-suspected entry below is CLOSED and both
`__extract_field_raw` and `__key_pos` are deleted in the
ordinary-layer dedup pass.)

## upstream 9bc53d5/hale#210: fallible Stream send/recv — missed in the 2026-07-15/16 migration pass, migrated 2026-08-03

The repo-wide migration to hale 9bc53d5's fallible
`Stream.send/recv` surface (see FRICTION § pond/websocket
"upstream 9bc53d5" and the 2026-07-15 CONTRACTS.md status note)
inventoried websocket, pq, and http/client — but never swept
agent/llm, whose `__stream_send` / `__stream_recv_bytes`
(anthropic.hl) drive a `std::io::tcp::Stream` locus directly on
the plaintext path. Both codegen gates failed from 2026-07-15
until today (`error not addressed: Stream.send is fallible`);
`hale check` stayed green throughout — fallibility disposition
enforcement is still codegen-only at v0.13.0, so the
green-check-≠-codegen-clean gotcha hid a 19-day breakage in a
lib whose gates weren't re-run.

Migration mirrors pq/stream.hl's dispositions: `tcp.send(wire)
or discard` (a failed send surfaces downstream as the length-0
recv in `__drain_stream`, matching the TLS path's negative
sentinel — every caller discards the send return), and
`tcp.recv_bytes(max) or b""` (errors collapse to the length-0
end-of-stream contract). The `std::io::tls::*` path-call sites
are untouched — still non-fallible Int-sentinel per the v0.13
stdlib surface. No public-surface change. Gates at hale
v0.13.0: check ok (0 warnings), test 2/2, echo-completion
builds.

Process lesson (recorded so the next breaking-stdlib pass
doesn't repeat it): the 07-15 site inventory was built by
grepping the libs the ICE reports named, not by sweeping the
repo for `Stream` construction — `hale test .` at the migration
compiler would have caught agent/llm on day one.

