## 2026-06-08 — or-fallback-interface-same-name-structural-trap — [CLOSED]

**Status:** [CLOSED — FIXED IN-LIB]

**Root cause of the `type Tool cannot satisfy interface Tool`
build break.** The previous source routed the proof-of-
unreachable `@form(vec).get` fallback through a helper fn whose
return type was the *interface*:

```hale
fn __noop_tool() -> Tool { return __NoopTool { }; }
let t = entries.get(i) or __noop_tool();
```

By 2026-06 the typechecker's `or <substitute>` arm *does* run
LocusRef → Interface coercion (the 2026-05-18 gap below is
closed). But that coercion path also fires a structural-impl
check, and — unlike the fn-arg call-site — the `or <substitute>`
arm has no `rhs_name != iface_name` identity guard
(`hale-types/src/check.rs`, the `interface_satisfied` block in
the `Or::Substitute` checker). When the substitute is *already*
the interface type `Tool`, the checker calls
`check_structural_impl("Tool", "Tool")`, which looks up the
first `Tool` as a *locus*, finds the interface instead, and
emits `type \`Tool\` cannot satisfy interface \`Tool\` — only
loci satisfy interfaces`. The helper-fn indirection was the
direct cause: it made the substitute's success type name
collide with the interface's name.

**Fix taken (smallest correct change).** Drop the `__noop_tool()`
helper and use the locus literal directly:

```hale
let t = entries.get(i) or __NoopTool { };
```

The substitute's type name is now `__NoopTool`, distinct from
the interface `Tool`, so the structural-impl check takes the
intended LocusRef → Interface path and succeeds. This is the
shape the 2026-05-18 entry *wanted* once coercion landed — the
helper was a stale workaround that became actively wrong.

---

## 2026-06-12 — or-substitute-interface-coercion-missing-at-codegen — [WORKAROUND IN-LIB]

**Status:** [GAP — WORKAROUND-DOCUMENTED]

Follow-on to the two closed entries below. The 2026-06-08 shape
`let t = entries.get(i) or __NoopTool { };` typechecks (the
`or <substitute>` checker runs LocusRef → Interface coercion)
but at hale HEAD (b716696) it is rejected at CODEGEN when the
example is built:

```
codegen error: unsupported in codegen v0: `or` substitute type
mismatch: expected Some(Interface("__lib_tools_interfaces_Tool")),
got Some(LocusRef("__lib_tools_registry___NoopTool"))
```

Minimal repro (verified 2026-06-12, /tmp probe): any
`@form(vec)` of an interface + `get(i) or ConcreteLocus { }`.
The coercion exists at typecheck but codegen v0's `or`-
substitute lowering compares nominal types with no coercion
step.

**Workaround taken (in-lib, both call sites):** hoist the
sentinel into an interface-typed binding before the loop —

```hale
let noop: Tool = __NoopTool { };
let t = entries.get(i) or noop;
```

The let-ascription coerces LocusRef → Interface up front, so
the substitute is already interface-typed at codegen. Verified:
lib typechecks and `examples/calc-tool` builds + runs with
documented output.

**Suggested upstream resolution.** Run the same LocusRef →
Interface coercion in codegen's `or`-substitute arm that the
typechecker already runs (or lower the coercion at typecheck so
codegen never sees the raw LocusRef).

**2026-07-04 retest (hale HEAD 400ac68): HALF-CLOSED.** The
coercion now works for plain fallible calls —
`let g: Iface = fallible_fn() or Concrete { };` builds and runs
(fresh probe) — but the `@form(vec)`-of-interface shape this
entry is about (`entries.get(i) or ConcreteLocus { }`) still
dies at codegen with the same mismatch (consistent with
upstream's note that `@form`-synthesized methods are outside the
new handler/substitute classifier). Hoisted-noop workaround
stays in `registry.hl`.

