# pond/crypto — friction log

The lib is fully unblocked as of v0.8.1. All stdlib-gap entries
(SHA-256, getrandom, bytes-builder, bitwise-NOT, cross-seed free-fn
dispatch) shipped 2026-05-17 and the workarounds collapsed to the
direct surface; the namespace-lotus contract deviation also retired.
Git log carries the historical trail.

Below: live observations that aren't blocking but may surface later.

## suspected-duplicate helpers

### nibble-char / nibble-value — [still open, re-checked 2026-06-12]

`hex.hl` declares two helpers — `nibble_char(n) -> String` (0..15
to one hex char) and `nibble_value(c) -> Int` (byte value of an
ASCII hex digit to 0..15, -1 otherwise). The stdlib's
`__json_hex_byte` / `__json_hex_nibble` /  `__json_hex_char` in
`runtime/stdlib/json.hl` are the same shape but remain
underscore-internal at HEAD — there is still no public
`std::text::hex`-style surface, so the local helpers stay. If a
future pond/json or pond/jwt lib lands, those helpers should
graduate upstream.

### char_at_byte — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

`hex.hl`'s `char_at_byte(s, i)` wrapper
(`std::bytes::at(std::bytes::from_string(s), i)` — one heap copy
per call) is retired: `std::str::byte_at_unchecked(s, i) -> Int`
shipped upstream 2026-05-26 (inline GEP+load, no copy, no bounds
check — caller guarantees `0 <= i < len(s)`). `hex_decode`'s two
call sites satisfy the bound from the loop structure (`i + 1 < n`
with `n` even), so the swap is safe. sign-demo passes, including
the hex round-trip.


## additive: sha512 / hmac_sha512 — 2026-07-04

Upstream shipped `std::crypto::{sha512, hmac_sha512}` (hale
`045577a`). This lib adds the matching thin delegates
`sha512(input)` / `hmac_sha512(key, message)` — additive
CONTRACTS.md extension, annotated inline in the surface block.
sign-demo unchanged and passing.

## unbounded-alloc triage — 2026-07-04

The one default-on warning (`hex_decode`'s `HexError` literal
"inside an unbounded loop") is FP-shaped: the loop is bounded
by the input string length and the `fail` exits it. No source
change.

*2026-08-03 re-baseline (hale v0.13.0):* 1 → **0 warnings** —
the classifier now models exactly the shape triaged above
(`fail` exits the loop). Clean with no source change.

## effects pilot — 2026-08-04: the lib carries certificates

First pond lib to adopt the hale v0.11.23+ effect-assertion
surface. All nine pure fns (`sha256` / `hmac_sha256` / `sha512`
/ `hmac_sha512` / `constant_time_eq` / `hex_encode` /
`hex_decode` / `nibble_char` / `nibble_value`) now carry
`@deterministic @no_syscall` — the stdlib crypto primitives they
delegate to are classified effect-free, so the certificates hold
under `hale check`'s fail-closed engine. `random_bytes` is
deliberately NOT annotated (it reaches
`std::os::getrandom` — `syscall` + `entropy`; that reach is its
purpose). Negative control verified before shipping: a
`@deterministic` mis-placed on `random_bytes` fails check with a
witness path (`must not reach entropy … [std::os::getrandom —
an entropy read]`), so the annotations are enforced, not
decorative. `hale verify crypto/` reports 0 findings. Pilot
verdict for the repo: the surface is cheap where a lib is
genuinely pure; roll out opportunistically, not as a mandate.

