# FRICTION — pond/http/client

Log of language / stdlib gaps, duplication suspicions, and
contract deviations encountered while building this lib.

**2026-06-12 sweep (hale HEAD `43300e5`):** lib typechecks
clean; `examples/get-demo/` builds and fetches a local
`python3 -m http.server` (status 200, body printed). Entry
deltas this pass: cross-seed `const` CLOSED (consts restored in
client.hl); the or-clause/`fail` entry partially lifted
(`or fail E { lit }` lowers; `err` still unbound in the
payload — helpers stay).

## Contract deviations vs CONTRACTS.md

### `Client` methods cannot be `fallible(HttpError)` — [CLOSED 2026-06-08]

**2026-06-08 resolution.** Migrated to the v0.8.1 two-channel rule
(#24 v0.2, commits `d565d6f` + `98910b9`). `Client.get` / `.post` /
`.request` now declare `fallible(HttpError)` directly and match the
free-fn surface in CONTRACTS.md verbatim. Removed the entire
workaround: the `last_kind` / `last_status` / `last_detail` scratch
params, the `last_error_kind()` / `last_error_status()` /
`last_error_detail()` accessors, the `__record_err` bridge, and the
`__client_{get,post,request}_fallible` free-fn mirrors (their bodies
folded straight into the methods). No deviation remains; the as-built
surface equals the contract. Callers address the value channel with
`or raise` / `or <substitute>` / an error-check fn.

**2026-05-27 (historical).** Upstream v0.8.1 narrowed the two-channel
rule so user-declared `fn` member fns can carry `fallible(E)` with
heap-bearing payloads; this entry tracked the pending source pass that
the 2026-06-08 migration above completed.

## Language / stdlib gaps

### `or` clause can't `fail` — [PARTIALLY LIFTED, re-tested 2026-06-12]

HEAD update: `or fail SomeError { ...literal fields... }` now
parses, typechecks, AND lowers — as long as the payload literal
doesn't reference `err`. Referencing the inner error
(`or fail HttpError { detail: err.kind }`) typechecks but dies
at codegen with `unknown identifier err` (fresh repro; same
finding logged in FRICTION.log
§ or-fail-err-binding, where it silently broke the lib until an
example forced codegen). So for translations that must carry
the inner error's fields — which is most of them — the
two-stage helper idiom below remains the shape:

```hale
fn __raise_connect(e: IoError, host: String, port: Int) -> Int fallible(HttpError) {
    fail HttpError { kind: "connect_failed", ... };
}

fn __dial(host: String, port: Int) -> Int fallible(HttpError) {
    let fd = std::io::tcp::connect(host, port)
        or (__raise_connect(err, host, port) or raise);
    return fd;
}
```

Remaining upstream ask: bind `err` inside the or-fail payload
literal (as `or handler(err)` already does); then each helper
collapses into its call site.

**2026-07-04 retest (hale HEAD 400ac68): err-binding still
missing** (fresh probe, same `unknown identifier err` at
codegen) — the two-stage helpers stay. But upstream now accepts
handlers that are THEMSELVES `fallible(E2)` with implicit
propagation, so the call shape simplifies from
`or (__raise_connect(err, host, port) or raise)` to
`or __raise_connect(err, host, port)` — the helper's failure
propagates through the enclosing fn's error path automatically.
Adopted in this pass (2026-07-04 cleanup leg).

### Cross-seed `const` lookups don't resolve at codegen — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

Probe at HEAD: `const GREETING: String = "...";` in a lib seed
resolves both cross-seed (`l::GREETING` from a consumer) and
intra-seed (via a lib fn) — builds and runs. The workaround
(inlining the literal at every use site) is retired:
`client.hl` re-declares `DEFAULT_USER_AGENT` /
`DEFAULT_MAX_BODY` consts and the three one-shot free fns
reference them. (The `Client` locus param default keeps its own
literal — param-default-from-const is untested and the locus
default is part of the contract text anyway.)

### `len()` overload across String and Bytes

`len()` works on both String (strlen-style) and Bytes (length-
prefix). The dispatch happens at the typechecker. This is
fine but worth noting — agents reaching for `b.length` or
`std::bytes::len(b)` won't find them.

## Duplicate-suspected helpers

### `__find_header` (wire.hl)

Duplicates `__http_request_header` from
`hale/runtime/stdlib/http.hl`. Same packed-CRLF block
shape, same case-insensitive lookup, same trim-leading-
whitespace logic. The stdlib version operates on
`std::http::Request.headers`; ours operates on a free String.

duplicate-suspected: `__find_header` — may belong in a shared
http-header util (perhaps `std::http::find_header(headers: String,
name: String) -> String` as a path-call sibling to
`std::http::header(req: Request, name: String)`).
(Re-checked 2026-06-12: stdlib http.hl now factors a shared
walker for the Request- and Response-side `header()` lookups,
but it stays underscore-internal and Request/Response-typed —
no public free-String surface yet, so this entry stays open.)

### URL parsing (url.hl)

`parse_url` is self-contained but generally useful — any other
pond lib that needs to address resources by URL (`pond/agent/llm`,
webhook receivers, `pond/sessions` for redirect-back, etc.)
will want the same Url shape.

duplicate-suspected: `parse_url` + the `Url` type — may belong
in a shared `pond/url/` or `pond/util/` lib so multiple HTTP-
adjacent libs don't each redeclare it.

### Connection-pool LRU (client.hl)

The fixed-cap parallel-array LRU in `Client` is a hand-rolled
shape. Any lib that holds a small set of long-lived resources
(file handles, DB connections, websockets) will want the same
shape.

duplicate-suspected: LRU pool — may belong in a future
`@form(lru_cache, max = N)` form or a `pond/util/lru/` lib.
The hand-rolled shape works at v1 but doesn't compose.

**2026-07-04: `@form(lru_cache, cap = N)` SHIPPED upstream —
evaluated for this pool and DECLINED.** Two structural
mismatches, both fatal for a pool of live fds:
(1) no `remove` — `__pool_take` hands ownership of an fd to the
caller and must vacate the slot, but the form's surface is only
put/get/contains/len (a get leaves the entry live, so one fd
could be handed out twice);
(2) no eviction hook — `put` over cap silently drops the LRU
entry, and a silently dropped cell that OWNS an open fd is an
fd leak (the hand-rolled evict path closes it first).
The form fits pure-value caches; resource pools need
take-semantics + an eviction callback. Upstream ask sharpened:
`remove(k)` + `put_evicting(x) -> S` (returning the displaced
cell) would make `@form(lru_cache)` pool-capable. Entry stays
open on that ask; the hand-rolled pool stays.

**Also triaged 2026-07-04:** the 12 default-on unbounded-alloc
warnings on `keepalive.hl` (`__FramedRead`/`__ChunkScan`/bytes
literals in the recv loops) and `client.hl`
(`__round_trip_with_retry`'s HttpError) are the classifier's
documented residual-FP class — loops runtime-bounded by
`max_body` / `max_attempts` in scratch-reclaimed frames. No
source change.

*2026-08-03 re-baseline (hale v0.13.0):* 12 → **2 warnings**.
Every keepalive.hl site cleared via the v0.11.12 analysis
improvements. Remaining: `client.hl:150` (the HttpError site
above, still accepted as triaged) and a NEW class on
`wire.hl:111` — "hot-path allocation: `recv_bytes` in a loop
allocates a fresh result buffer each iteration", recommending
`recv_into` + a reused `BytesBuilder`. Valid advice, not taken:
this lib is FROZEN (promoted to `std::http` in hale ≥ v0.11.4;
vendored copy kept for older pins), and older pins predate
`recv_into`. The live surface to optimize is the stdlib client.

## Out-of-scope notes

### Connection pooling is mostly cosmetic at v1

Because we send `Connection: close` (matching the stdlib's
`std::http::write_response` shape), the server closes the
socket after every response. The cached fd in our pool would be
a dead fd — actual reuse needs `Connection: keep-alive` plus a
post-response read-eof check before re-using a cached fd. Left
as v1.x work; the param slots are wired so flipping it doesn't
need a params-block change.

### Retry backoff is fixed at 50ms doubling

`std::time::sleep` takes a `Duration`, and Duration arithmetic
at runtime (`backoff_ms * 2` and then turning that back into a
Duration literal) is awkward — Hale doesn't have an
`int_to_duration(ns)` primitive that I could find. We loop with
a hardcoded `50ms` sleep at v1; the `backoff_ms` field is
updated for diagnostic visibility only. A future revision should
either thread `Duration` arithmetic or expose
`std::time::sleep_ns(n: Int)` for parametric backoff.


