# pond/jobs — friction log

Gaps, suspicions, deviations encountered building pond/jobs/.
Per pond/README.md § Design rules: contract deviations get
logged here and reflected back in CONTRACTS.md.

**2026-06-12 status.** pond/sqlite became a real `@ffi` driver
(upstream WS4 verdict — no stdlib primitive was ever needed), and
HEAD `43300e5` (post-audit 2026-06-11) closed the codegen gaps
that shaped this lib's stub era. This pass un-stubbed everything:
the queue ops are `fallible(JobError)` member fns on `Queue`, the
contract's locus-ref/interface params are real, the free-fn shim
(`query.hl`) is deleted, and `examples/email-worker/` drives real
jobs through a real SQLite file. Entries below are stamped with
their resolution; items 5, 6, 7, 8, 14, 15, 16 remain open.

## 1. BLOCKED (transitive): pond/sqlite — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

pond/sqlite was BLOCKED on a stdlib primitive that turned out to
be unnecessary (`@ffi("c")` was the surface all along — see
`FRICTION.log § F.1`). With the real driver in place, every
queue op runs real SQL. The `Pool.params.simulate` stub-mode
switch and its `simulate_kind` / `simulate_payload_prefix`
companions are **deleted**; workers always run the real
dequeue → invoke → ack/fail loop. Evidence:
`examples/email-worker/` enqueues 10 + 1 jobs, drains them
through 2 workers, and verifies `done=10 failed=1 ready=0` in the
actual table (externally confirmed with a python sqlite3 client).

## 2. Deviation: Locus methods can't declare `fallible(E)` — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

v0.8.1 narrowed the two-channel rule (#24 v0.2) so user-declared
`fn` member fns carry `fallible(E)`; the source migration waited
for the sqlite unblock. Done: `enqueue` / `dequeue` / `ack` /
`fail` are member fns on `Queue` declaring `fallible(JobError)`
(queue.hl), restoring CONTRACTS.md verbatim. The free-fn surface
in `query.hl` is deleted. Call sites read `q.enqueue(kind, ...)`.
Evidence: `hale check jobs/` ok; email-worker demo round-trips.

## 3. Deviation: Locus refs can't sit in another locus's params — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

Retested at HEAD `43300e5`: locus-typed params — including
cross-seed qualified ones — typecheck, lower, AND run.
`Queue.params.db: sqlite::Db` and `Pool.params.queue: Queue` are
now exactly the CONTRACTS.md shape; the `db_path: String`
primitive-identity shadow is gone. Probed lifecycle semantics:
the param holds a REF — when the holder dissolves, the referenced
locus is NOT cascade-dissolved (a Db passed into a short-lived
holder stays usable after the holder exits), so the
lifecycle-orphaning concern that motivated the old workaround
does not materialize. The caller owns the injected locus's
lifetime and must keep it alive while the holder lives.

A child can also hold a ref to its own parent (`Worker { pool:
self }` inside `Pool.birth()`) and call member fns back through
it — load-bearing for item 15's workaround.

## 5. Worker exit shape (was: stub-mode worker shape) — open, refreshed 2026-06-12

The `simulate` switch is deleted (item 1). What remains true:
there is still **no parent→worker stop signal** (see item 14), so
`Worker.run()` needs a structured exit condition of its own. Two
bounded shapes ship today, both surfaced on Pool:

- `max_jobs_per_worker: Int = 0` — exit after N handler
  invocations (the original bounded shape).
- `exit_on_empty: Bool = false` — **new**: exit when `dequeue`
  reports `kind="empty"` (queue drained). This is the batch-shape
  exit the email-worker demo uses; daemons leave it false.

Both are additive params beyond the CONTRACTS.md sketch; fold
into the contract or drop when a real stop primitive lands.

## 6. Deviation: Job has no `id` field (CONTRACTS.md) — open (deviation now ACTIVE), refreshed 2026-06-12

The contract spells `Job { kind, payload, attempt, max_attempts }`
but `ack(job_id)` / `fail(job_id, retry)` take an id the worker
can only get from `dequeue()`. During the stub era this was
latent; now it's load-bearing: **as built, `Job` carries
`id: Int = 0`** (the queue row id, stamped by dequeue; 0 = not
from the queue), and the worker issues `ack(j.id)` /
`fail(j.id, retry)` with it.

**Proposed CONTRACTS.md amendment** (unchanged): add `id: Int`
to `Job` — the simplest of the options previously listed.

## 7. duplicate-suspected: DbError→JobError bridge helpers — NARROWED 2026-07-04 (was: open, refreshed 2026-06-12)

The old `__bridge_db_*` free fns became the `__stash_*` member-fn
family on `Queue` (an `or` handler can't itself be fallible — see
item 16 — so cross-payload error conversion goes stash-then-check).
The duplication is now CONFIRMED, not just suspected:
`migrations/sqlite_driver.hl` ships a near-identical `__stash_*`
family. Still advisory — a shared helper needs either generics
over the payload type or a dedicated seed, neither available/
warranted yet.

**2026-07-04 update:** the two families are no longer twins.
jobs' stash is GONE (item 16 closed — direct fallible bridges);
migrations' stash STAYS, for a structural reason the fix doesn't
touch: `SqliteDriver`'s methods are non-fallible by
`db::DbDriver` contract (ok/err-packed results), so there is no
enclosing error path for a fallible handler to propagate into.
What remains duplicated across libs is only the thin "fail with
kind/detail from a DbError" translation shape — below the
threshold for a shared seed. Entry stays advisory.

## 8. duplicate-suspected: Pool's on_failure restart-loop — open, refreshed 2026-06-12

Unchanged: `Pool.on_failure` open-codes `restart(w)` —
pond/supervisor's `one_for_one`. Revisit when Pool vendors
supervisor.

## 9. SQL string-building (no `db::bind_*` available) — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

The sqlite driver's `prepare` / `bind_text` / `bind_int` / `step`
/ `finalize` are real now, and `enqueue` uses them: `kind` /
`payload` / `max_attempts` travel as `?1`/`?2`/`?3` bind
parameters — externally-sourced values never touch the SQL text,
and the planned `__sql_escape` helper was never needed. Int row
ids in dequeue/ack/fail WHERE clauses are spliced via
`to_string(id)` (no injection surface in an Int; the values come
from our own AUTOINCREMENT column). The `table` name remains
spliced by design — it's configuration, not data (same stance as
the contract's `table: String` param).

## 10. Empty-body language gap — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

Probed at HEAD `43300e5`: empty lifecycle bodies (`birth() { }`,
`run() { }`, `drain() { }`, `dissolve() { }`) parse and build.
The `let _ = self.workers;` placeholders are removed.

## 11. ~~HARD BLOCKER~~: codegen v0 rejects qualified type names + qualified struct literals — **CLOSED 2026-06-12 (WS3.4)**

Fixed upstream (post-audit WS3.4). At HEAD `43300e5`, qualified
cross-seed types in fn/method signatures and params
(`db: sqlite::Db`, `fn __stash_row(e: sqlite::DbError) ->
sqlite::Row`) and qualified struct literals in expression position
(`sqlite::ExecResult { }`, `sqlite::Db { path: ... }`) all lower
and run from binaries that import the lib two hops out. Evidence:
queue.hl is built from exactly these shapes and
`examples/email-worker/` builds and runs. This was the single
most impactful friction in the chain; its closure is what made
this whole pass possible.

## 12. Codegen v0 rejects `-> () fallible(E)` return type — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

Closed upstream by `6beb1be` (FUv0.8.2 #6); source flip done this
pass: `ack` / `fail` declare `() fallible(JobError)` per the
contract (the Bool workaround is gone). Bonus retest: `or discard`
now works on a `()`-fallible call at HEAD (the old "got `()`"
rejection is fixed). NOTE (from the sqlite pass, still true): the
*non*-fallible explicit `-> ()` form still trips "tuple type must
have at least 2 elements" — omit the return type on non-fallible
unit methods.

## 13. `fail` keyword vs `fn fail()` shadowing — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

Probed at HEAD: a member fn named `fail` coexists with the `fail`
keyword — `fn fail(job_id: Int, retry: Bool) -> ()
fallible(JobError)` containing `fail JobError { ... }` parses,
builds, and runs. The public surface is now `q.fail(id, retry)`
per CONTRACTS.md verbatim; the `fail_job` rename (and its
proposed contract amendment) is withdrawn.

## 14a. `hale build <lib>/` fails for any library — resolved upstream, refreshed 2026-06-12

Now the documented behavior at HEAD: `hale check <libdir>/`
typechecks a main-less lib; `hale build` is for binaries
(examples). No longer a friction, kept for the record.

## 14. Worker shutdown signal is missing — open, refreshed 2026-06-12

Unchanged in substance: no parent→worker "stop now" channel
(worker declares no bus; cross-thread field reads are unblessed).
The bounded shapes from item 5 (`max_jobs`, `exit_on_empty`) are
the only structured exits. When a stop primitive (or
mailbox-shutdown sugar) lands, `Worker.run()`'s loop drops the
bounded shape with no contract change.

## 15. NEW (2026-06-12): interface values can't be re-injected into another interface-typed param

`Pool.params.handler: JobHandler` works (interface-typed params
hold values now — F.20 progressed), but **copying** that value
onward fails: `Worker { handler: self.handler }` is rejected with

```
type error: type `JobHandler` cannot satisfy interface
`JobHandler` — only loci satisfy interfaces
```

— the satisfaction check wants a concrete locus type and has no
identity case for an already-interface-typed value. Workaround
shipped: Worker holds a `pool: Pool` back-ref (legal per item 3's
parent-self note) and dispatches via `Pool.run_job(j)`, which
calls `self.handler.invoke(j)` one hop from the injection site.
Surface cost: none (Worker is internal). Proposed upstream fix:
allow interface-typed values to flow into params of the same (or
a structurally-narrower) interface type.

**2026-07-04 retest (hale HEAD 400ac68): still rejected**, same
"only loci satisfy interfaces" diagnostic. Back-ref workaround
stays.

## 16. `or handler(err)` handlers can't be fallible — stash-bridge pattern + reentrancy caveat — **CLOSED 2026-07-04**

**Closed:** hale 400ac68 accepts handlers that are themselves
`fallible(E2)`, with implicit propagation through the enclosing
fn's error path (upstream changelog names this lib's stash idiom
as the motivating case). `queue.hl` now bridges DbError→JobError
with direct fallible member fns (`__db_int` / `__db_exec` /
`__db_row` / `__db_row_or_empty` / the `*_fin` variants that
finalize the stmt before failing); the `err_kind`/`err_detail`
stash params, `__clear`, `__check`, and the whole `__stash_*`
family are deleted. The non-reentrancy caveat below dies with
the stash (queue ops no longer write shared error state);
`schema_error` alone survives, because `birth()` is a lifecycle
body and still can't be fallible. Verified: `hale check jobs/`
clean; email-worker demo runs 10 delivered + 1 dead-lettered
after retry, exit 0. Original entry preserved below.

## [historical] 16-original. NEW (2026-06-12): `or handler(err)` handlers can't be fallible — stash-bridge pattern + reentrancy caveat

Probed at HEAD: a disposition handler declaring `fallible(E2)` is
rejected ("fallback type `Int fallible(JobError)` does not match
success type `Int`"), so DbError→JobError conversion can't
`fail` from inside the handler. Pattern used instead (queue.hl):
`__stash_*` member fns record the first DbError on
`self.err_kind` / `self.err_detail` and return a sentinel; the op
body then runs `self.__check() or raise` to convert the stash
into a `fail JobError`. Consequences worth knowing:

- `Queue.params` carries private bookkeeping fields
  (`schema_error`, `err_kind`, `err_detail`) beyond the contract
  params — same precedent as sqlite's `conn_handle`.
- The stash makes each queue op **non-reentrant**: two truly
  parallel callers of the same Queue locus (e.g. workers pinned
  to different cores by the consuming app's `placement { }`)
  could interleave stash writes. Cooperative placement (the
  default, and what the demo uses) serializes the calls. If a
  consumer pins workers, give each its own Queue over its own
  `sqlite::Db` (file-backed path) — the rows are shared via the
  file, the stash is not.


## unbounded-alloc triage — 2026-07-04

One default-on warning: `Worker::__on_dequeue_err` returns a
sentinel `Job { }` per failed dequeue poll inside the worker's
run loop — real accumulation, but at poll rate with an
all-default struct (~tens of KB/day at 100 ms polls). Accepted
for now; the clean fix (activation-boundary retire for plain
fields) is staged upstream in notes/anchor-retirement.md.
Revisit when that lands or if a workload runs workers hot for
weeks.

*2026-08-03 re-baseline (hale v0.13.0):* 1 → **7 warnings**.
The `Job { }` site above is unchanged (still accepted). Six NEW
sites in `queue.hl` (`dequeue`/`ack`/`fail`): the `fail
JobError { ... }` literals and their string-concat details, now
flagged as "in a fn invoked unboundedly" because the callers are
Worker's run-loop poll and bus-reached paths. Same accepted
class as the Job sentinel: an error payload per FAILED
poll/ack, allocated in the caller's region at poll rate —
tens of KB/day, reclaimed at worker dissolve. (Contrast
migrations' identical-looking fail-literals, which sat inside
`while` loops the `fail` exits and are now modeled clean —
these six are flagged for the caller's loop, not their own, so
the v0.11.12 modeling correctly does NOT clear them.) Accepted
at parity with the sentinel entry; same revisit trigger
(anchor-retirement, or hot-for-weeks workers — a worker could
also acknowledge with `@unbounded` on the run hook if the
count should stop appearing in the gate).

