# pond/metrics — FRICTION

Gaps, suspicions, and deviations from CONTRACTS.md surfaced
while building this lib.

## 2026-06-12 status note

Re-tested every blocked-on entry against upstream HEAD
`43300e5` (post-audit 2026-06-11). All four are closed: the
multi-file split is back (seven per-concern files replace the
consolidated `metrics.hl`), locus-field default-init works,
the `Foo { reg: self }` codegen gap is superseded by the m90
typecheck rule, and consumers no longer need a parallel
`math/matrix` import just to build against this lib. The
design-question / deviation entries below remain current.

## RESOLVED 2026-06-12: cross-seed multi-file resolution

**Closed at upstream HEAD `43300e5` (post-audit 2026-06-11,
WS3.4-family pass-A registration fix).** The lib is re-split
into the seven per-concern files the original design called
for (`types.hl`, `storage.hl`, `labels.hl`, `helpers.hl`,
`registry.hl`, `handles.hl`, `endpoint.hl`); the consolidated
`metrics.hl` is gone. Evidence: `hale check metrics/` passes
("ok: 7 file(s) typechecked") and the downstream consumer
(`examples/exposition-demo/`, which imports the seed and
touches types declared across four of the seven files) builds
and runs with all format checks passing — the exact
configuration that previously raised

```
codegen error: unsupported in codegen v0: unknown type name
`__lib_metrics_<file_stem>_<Name>` in signature
```

Historical context (for archaeology): the importer's codegen
pass A0/A1 didn't register cross-file declarations before
signature-resolving them, so any consumer import of a
multi-file seed failed on every cross-file type reference
inside the lib. The 2026-05-27 workaround consolidated all
seven concerns into one `metrics.hl` so the mangler emitted
`__lib_metrics_metrics_<Name>` uniformly. That workaround is
now retired; no source changes were needed beyond the file
split itself.

## RESOLVED 2026-06-12: default-init of locus-typed param field

**Gate verified lifted at upstream HEAD `43300e5`.** Probe
(/tmp, 2026-06-12): `params { codes: CodeMap = CodeMap { }; }`
with `CodeMap` a `@form(hashmap)` locus, followed by
`self.codes.set(...)` / `.len()` / `.get(...)` — builds, runs,
and reads back the written cell correctly. No segfault. The
struct-literal default now runs the nested locus's heap-init
path.

**Source intentionally unchanged.** The Registry's
`store: MetricMap` and `histograms: HistogramList` stay
required (no default) because the locked CONTRACTS.md surface
declares them default-less; the two-step ctor remains the
documented shape:

```hale
let store = metrics::MetricMap { };
let hl    = metrics::HistogramList { };
let reg   = metrics::Registry {
    namespace: "myapp", store: store, histograms: hl
};
```

If a future CONTRACTS.md pass wants one-step
`metrics::Registry { namespace: "myapp" }` construction,
defaulting the two fields is now safe; that is a contract
change, not a friction workaround.

Historical: pre-fix, the struct-literal default didn't run the
nested locus's heap-init, so first access wrote into
uninitialized memory and segfaulted. The shortener
experiment's `codes: CodeMap = CodeMap { }` pattern, flagged
latent in the 2026-05-27 note, is no longer hazardous.

## SUPERSEDED 2026-06-12: `Foo { reg: self, ... }` in a method-returning-locus body

**Closed as superseded — the surrounding shape is now rejected
by language rule, not by a codegen gap.** Probe (/tmp,
2026-06-12, upstream HEAD `43300e5`): a locus method declaring
`fn make() -> Child` fails at *typecheck* with

```
type error: method `Parent.make` declares return type `Child`
— methods on loci may not return locus values.
```

(hale v0.8.2 m90 / CQRS no-locus-return enforcement; the
diagnostic suggests parent-child `accept`, bus topics, or
delegation, and notes free fns can still return loci). The old
`codegen error: unsupported in codegen v0: expression form
Discriminant(3)` is unreachable because the construct never
reaches codegen.

This lib already follows the mandated shape: `counter` /
`gauge` / `histogram` are free fns taking `Registry` as the
explicit first arg (per the CONTRACTS.md 2026-06-08 CQRS
note), and the returned handles carry *slot references*
(`store: MetricMap`, `histograms: HistogramList`) rather than
the whole Registry. Free fns may legally return
`Counter { registry: reg, ... }` if a future pass wants
back-ref handles; the slot-ref shape is kept because it is
narrower and nothing needs the back-ref.

## RESOLVED 2026-06-12: cross-seed `mat::Matrix` resolution chain

**Gate verified lifted at upstream HEAD `43300e5`.** Probe
(/tmp, 2026-06-12): a consumer seed that imports `metrics`
but does **not** import `math/matrix`, registers a counter,
and renders — builds and runs cleanly. The lib's internal
`mat::Matrix` references (factory signature in `handles.hl`,
`floats_to_csv` in `helpers.hl`) now resolve against the
lib's own alias table instead of the consumer's, so the old

```
codegen error: unsupported in codegen v0:
  qualified type `mat::Matrix` not in stdlib path-renames table
```

no longer fires on consumers who skip the matrix import.

Remaining (by design, not a bug): a consumer that calls
`metrics::histogram(reg, name, buckets, labels)` must still
vendor + import `math/matrix` to *construct* the `buckets`
Matrix value — that is the explicit v1 "no transitive deps"
rule from `pond/README.md`, now correctly scoped to actual
use rather than to mere importing.

## duplicate-suspected: Prometheus text-format renderer

The Prometheus text exposition format is well-defined and
reusable: `# TYPE name kind\n`, sample lines with
`{label="value"}` blocks, histogram triple
(`_bucket` / `_sum` / `_count`). The rendering helpers
(`prom_full_name`, `prom_escape_label_value`,
`render_type_line`, `render_sample`, `render_labels`,
`render_histogram` — all in `helpers.hl` since the 2026-06-12
re-split) are likely to surface in a parallel
`pond/observability/...` lib or in a future
`std::text::prometheus` module. Candidate for promotion once a
second consumer materializes; staying inside the metrics seed
for now per the "only edit pond/metrics/" rule.

## duplicate-suspected: tab-separated string accessors — NARROWED 2026-07-04

**2026-07-04:** the histogram half of this family is GONE —
`HistogramData.bounds_csv`/`counts_csv` migrated to
`bounded[Float; 32]` / `bounded[Int; 33]` (the sanctioned
fixed-capacity shape since hale shipped `bounded[T; N]`), so
`float_at`, `int_at`, `int_csv_inc_at`, `zero_counts_csv`, and
`floats_to_csv` are deleted. What survives is `nth_tab_token` +
`len_floats` serving `render_labels` over the contract-locked
`Labels { kv: String }` shape. A Labels migration to bounded
pairs is possible but is a CONTRACTS.md delta rippling through
every metric constructor — deferred until a workload pushes
(logged here as the candidate). Original entry:

The `nth_tab_token`, `len_floats`, `int_at`, `float_at`,
`int_csv_inc_at` family in `helpers.hl` re-implements a tiny
slice of what a parametric `Vec<T>` API would give for free.
Two parallel uses already live in this lib (bucket-bounds
Floats and cumulative-count Ints); the same shape almost
certainly already lives in `pond/router`'s path-param decode,
in `pond/sessions`' kv block, and elsewhere. Candidate for a
`std::str::csv_split` / `std::str::csv_at_int` /
`std::str::csv_at_float` family, or for a small
`pond/text/csv` lib. Staying inline here pending a second
consumer to confirm the shape.

## design-question: storing locus refs in `@form(...)` cells

`@form(hashmap)` and `@form(vec)` cells must be value-shape
structs per `spec/semantics.md § Slot restrictions (v1)` — no
LocusRef cells. That's why histograms' bucket bounds + counts
live as serialized tab-strings inside the `HistogramData`
struct rather than as embedded Matrix refs. Every observe()
pays a parse + re-serialize cost (O(B) per call where B is the
bucket count). For the contract's "single instance per app"
shape with O(10) buckets per histogram and infrequent observe
rates, this is fine; surface here if a hot-path workload
demands per-bucket integer-array storage. The natural lift is
a per-histogram `@form(vec)` child locus owning the counts
buffer, parented to Registry via `as_parent_for` —
`spec/forms.md` doesn't yet document the composition pattern.

## design-question: histogram monotonicity as an inline closure

The task brief said "encode as a `closure { sum(... bucket >= ...)
~~ ... }` invariant on the Histogram locus." The closure-
assertion grammar (`spec/grammar.ebnf § closure_decl`) is
`expression ~~ expression within expression` — a single
numeric-comparison shape with optional `sum(...)` accumulators
per `crates/hale-codegen/tests/fixtures/examples/41-closure-
accumulator/`. There's no surface that expresses "for every
pair (i, i+1), counts[i+1] >= counts[i]" directly inside the
assertion; the pair-quantifier shape would require either a
new accumulator vocabulary (`all_pairs(...)`, `monotonic(...)`)
or a value-channel computation feeding a single scalar into
the assertion.

This lib chose the latter: a free fn (`count_out_of_order`)
recomputes the invariant in observe() after each update,
folds it into a single `self.out_of_order: Int` field, and
fires `violate buckets_monotonic;` when it's non-zero. The
closure declaration uses `epoch inline` (no auto-fire,
assertion-less) per `spec/grammar.ebnf § closure_decl`
v1.x-VIOLATE and `spec/semantics.md § Inline closure
violation`:

```hale
closure buckets_monotonic {
    captures: out_of_order;
    epoch inline;
}
```

The `captures:` clause makes `out_of_order` part of the audit
snapshot routed to the parent's `on_failure(c, err)`. This is
the closest the v1 closure grammar supports to a "buckets are
monotonic" structural assertion — if a future spec adds a
`pair_sum` or `monotonic` accumulator, this should migrate to
the assertion form.

## deviation: namespace prefix injected at render() time

`Registry.namespace` is consulted only in `render()` (via
`prom_full_name`), not at registration. Two consequences:

1. Changing `namespace` between registrations and a render
   would change the rendered names without re-registering.
   The contract surface ("namespace: String = """) reads as
   set-at-construction, so this matches the spec; the
   render-time application is documented here.
2. Composite keys in `store` use the bare `name` (without
   the namespace), so a counter named "requests" registered
   under namespace "a" collides with one named "requests"
   under namespace "b" on the *same* Registry. Single-
   Registry-per-app is the documented use, so this isn't
   reachable in practice.

## deviation: histogram observe() walks the buckets linearly — HALVED 2026-07-04

`Histogram.observe` walks bounds[] front-to-back looking for
the first bound `>= v`. O(B) per observe. The Prometheus
convention is B in the 5–20 range so linear is the right
shape; if a workload demands large-B histograms, a binary
search would slot in cleanly here.

**2026-07-04:** the expensive half of the O(B) — the
tab-string parse + re-serialize + fresh String per observe —
is gone with the bounded[T; N] migration (counts update via
the in-place `set` intrinsic on the local copy, then one
`vec.set` write-back; capacity 32 buckets, clamped at
creation). Registry.render() also moved off the O(cap×len)
`key_at` walk onto `for e in store.entries` (hale's batched
form-iteration surface). Exposition-demo passes all format
checks unchanged.

## design-question: render() doesn't sort metric series

`render()` walks `store` in `@form(hashmap)` hash-table order
(per `spec/forms.md` `key_at`/`entry_at`). Order is
deterministic for a given table state but *not* lexicographic.
Prometheus consumers don't require lex-sorted output (the
exposition format doesn't mandate ordering) but some operators
rely on diff-friendly stable output. Sorting before render
would add an O(N log N) step and a temporary buffer; deferred
until a workload demonstrates the friction.

## deviation: `MetricsEndpoint.handle` ignores `req.path`

The endpoint responds to every request (any method, any path)
with the metrics dump. The CONTRACTS.md surface
(`fn handle(ctx: Context) -> Response`) implies routing-aware
dispatch, but `std::http::Handler` (v1) takes a `Request`, not
a `Context` — there's no `Context` type / `RouteParams` in
stdlib yet (those live in `pond/router`, a separate Tier 1
lib). The natural composition is: mount `MetricsEndpoint` at a
`pond/router` route bound to `GET /metrics`. The endpoint
itself stays unconditional so it works both standalone and
behind a router.

## unbounded-alloc triage — 2026-08-03 (first recorded)

Two default-on warnings at hale v0.13.0: `render_labels`'
string-concat loop (`helpers.hl:132/134`). FP-shaped: bounded
by the metric's label-pair count (single digits in practice),
building the exposition line the caller returns. This lib is
FROZEN (promoted wholesale to `std::metrics` in hale ≥ v0.11.8)
so no source change on principle; the live surface is the
stdlib's.


