## 14. NEW (2026-07-06) — UPSTREAM BUG: form-vec-growing free-fn factory segfaults after a caught Migrator failure — [OPEN, workaround in tests/example]

**2026-08-03 retest (hale v0.13.0): STILL SEGFAULTS, 5/5 runs**
(reconstructed per the in-tree recipe below: real migrations+db
seeds, FakeDriver, one caught bad_set failure, then a
`std_set()` factory — exit 139 every run, crash inside the
factory call). Newly established: this is a **SEPARABLE bug**,
not a manifestation of the § pond/ml/neural method-frame family
it was filed under — that parent repro is FIXED at v0.13.0
(20/20 clean) while this one still crashes. **Reported upstream
same day as hale-lang/hale#375** (full repro inline, the
reduction matrix, and the separability finding; the still-open
ml/neural zero-reads manifestation is cross-referenced there as
a possible shared root). Workaround unchanged and still binding.

**Symptom (hale HEAD `400ac68`, deterministic 3/3 runs).** After
ANY `Migrator` run-op failure is caught by an `or` handler in
`fn main()`, the next call to a free fn that builds-and-grows a
`@form(vec)` locus (e.g. a `fn std_set() -> Migrations` test
factory doing `Migrations { }` + `add()` ×3 + return) segfaults
— classic delayed-corruption profile of the OPEN method-frame
heap-corruption family (§ pond/ml/neural), but a NEW
manifestation: the poisoning happens on the ERROR PATH of a
locus method, in `fn main()`'s frame, with no method-frame locus
creation anywhere.

**Probe matrix (all /tmp-style probes, 3+ runs each, same day):**

| shape                                                        | result |
|--------------------------------------------------------------|--------|
| factory (grows form-vec) called BEFORE any failure           | clean |
| Migrator failure caught → factory called after               | SEGFAULT 3/3 |
| ...failure kind varied (bad_set empty / bad_set dup / migration_failed mid-step) | SEGFAULT 3/3 each |
| Migrator failure caught → set built INLINE in main (literal + add() calls) | clean |
| toy fallible locus (no params) fail → factory                | clean |
| toy + partial fail-literal (defaulted fields) → factory      | clean |
| toy + fail through 2 nested `or raise` hops → factory        | clean |
| toy holding @form(vec) param, fail → factory                 | clean |
| toy holding db::DbDriver interface param, fail → factory     | clean |
| toy holding BOTH params, fail → factory                      | clean |

So no toy reduction reproduces it yet — the trigger needs
something more of Migrator's failing chain (suspects: the
form-vec's SYNTHESIZED `get` + `or <substitute>` reads inside
`__validate`/`entry()` executed in the frames the failure then
unwinds). In-tree repro recipe: take tests/migrator_test.hl,
extract any one failure scenario plus a `std_set()`-style factory
called after it — crashes every run; the shipped test builds
every set inline instead and passes 3/3.

**Workaround (shipped, and binding on consumers):** after any
caught Migrator failure, do NOT build migration sets (or any
@form(vec) locus) via a free-fn factory — construct inline in a
`fn main()` / lifecycle frame (literal + `add()` calls). Noted in
the test header, the Migrations doc comment, and the README.

**When this unblocks:** collapse the inline set duplication in
tests/migrator_test.hl back to one `std_set()` helper, drop the
SHAPE RULE header note, and delete this entry alongside the
ml/neural blocker.

---

## 15. NEW (2026-07-06): design note — tracking writes avoid exec_params by design (for now)

The v2 tracking INSERT/DELETE interpolate `version` (Int) and
`name` (via `__quote()`, '' doubling) into the SQL text instead
of using the interface's `exec_params`. Deliberate: building a
`db::Args` (@form(vec)) INSIDE a Migrator method frame and
passing it into another locus's method is precisely the shape of
the open method-frame fresh-locus heap corruption
(§ pond/ml/neural — and entry 14 shows this lib's failure paths
already brush that family). Migration names are
developer-authored constants, not user input, so `__quote()` is
a correctness fix (the v1 code broke on apostrophes), not an
injection defense. When the upstream corruption family closes,
switching to `exec_params($1,$2)` is a two-line change; the v1
injection-shaped concatenation is already gone either way.

