# pond/sqlite — friction log

This file was the **primary deliverable** for this lib during its
BLOCKED era (2026-05-16 → 2026-06-12): `pond/sqlite/` shipped stub
bodies while waiting on a stdlib primitive that — it turned out —
was never needed. As of **2026-06-12 the lib is a real, working
SQLite driver** built as a pure `@ffi("c")` library (upstream WS4
verdict, `hale/notes/sqlite-via-ffi-recipe.md`). The entries below
are kept as the historical record, each stamped with its
resolution.

Format follows the friction-log convention in
`notes/agent-onboarding/app-dev-brief.md`: one entry per gap, with
a short tag, a description, a reproducer (where applicable), and a
proposed resolution.

---

## F.1 — `no-stdlib-sqlite-primitive` — **CLOSED 2026-06-12 (premise refuted)**

**Tag:** `no-stdlib-sqlite-primitive`
**Severity:** was blocking — defined the entire lib's status.

**Resolution (2026-06-12).** The premise — "none of open / prepare
/ bind / step / finalize / close can be expressed without a stdlib
primitive, and AGENTS.md's *don't edit crates/* rule forbids adding
one" — was **incorrect**. Hale ships `@ffi("c")` (`spec/ffi.md`,
opening line: *"No stdlib expansion is required to bind a new
library."*) as a **library-author** surface that never touches
`crates/`. The upstream post-audit pass (WS4, 2026-06-11) called
this out explicitly and shipped the recipe
(`hale/notes/sqlite-via-ffi-recipe.md`); the language repo will
**not** add `std::db::sqlite::*`. Where the original entry went
wrong: its reproducer grepped `runtime/` and `spec/` for sqlite
symbols — it never looked for a *generic* C-binding surface, so it
missed `spec/ffi.md` entirely.

The driver is now implemented in-lib: `glue.c` (thin
`lotus_sqlite_*` shims over `sqlite3_*`), `ffi.hl` (`@ffi("c")`
extern decls), `hale.toml` (`[ffi] csrc = ["glue.c"], link =
["sqlite3"]`, auto-picked-up by importers), and `db.hl` (the Hale
wrapper applying `fallible(DbError)` over the C sentinels).
Evidence: `examples/kv-demo/` performs a real
CREATE/INSERT/SELECT round-trip against
`/tmp/pond-sqlite-kv-demo.db` —

```
[kv-demo] SELECT one: greeting	hello, pond
[kv-demo] DbError: kind=constraint sqlite_code=19 detail=UNIQUE constraint failed: kv.k
```

— and the db file reads back correctly from an external sqlite3
client. Build prerequisite: `libsqlite3-dev` on the build host
(deploy hosts need only the runtime `.so`), same class of system
dep as the existing `-lssl`/`-lcrypto`.

The proposed `std::db::sqlite::*` path-call surface that filled
most of this entry is retired unimplemented; its signatures live on
almost verbatim as the `lotus_sqlite_*` glue surface (which the
recipe had, in turn, lifted from here).

---

## F.2 — `contracts-md-locus-methods-fallible` — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

**Tag:** `contracts-md-locus-methods-fallible`
**Severity:** was a deviation; CONTRACTS.md needed no amendment.

**History.** The lib was authored under the pre-v0.8.1 blanket
"locus methods can't be fallible" rule, so the SQL surface shipped
as free fns (`db::exec(conn, sql)`) shadowing the contract's
method shape. v0.8.1 narrowed the rule (#24 v0.2, `d565d6f` +
`98910b9`): user-declared `fn` member fns carry `fallible(E)`;
only substrate-facing surfaces (lifecycle, modes, closure
assertions, bus handlers) stay rejected.

**Resolution (2026-06-12).** The F.1 implementation pass restored
the contract verbatim: `query.hl`'s free-fn shim is **deleted**,
and `exec` / `query_one` / `query_all` / `prepare` / `bind_text` /
`bind_int` / `step` / `finalize` are member fns on `Db` declaring
`fallible(DbError)` directly (db.hl). Call sites read
`conn.exec(sql) or raise;` as CONTRACTS.md always specified.
Evidence: `hale check sqlite/` → `ok: 3 file(s) typechecked`;
kv-demo exercises every method through the full `or
handler(err)` disposition surface.

---

## F.3 — `consider-flat-file-kv-fallback` (considered, declined) — **moot since 2026-06-12**

**Tag:** `consider-flat-file-kv-fallback`
**Severity:** advisory.

**Description.** During the BLOCKED era the prompt suggested a
file-based fallback store (flat-file KV) so the lib could ship
*something* working. Declined: the CONTRACTS.md surface speaks SQL,
and a flat-file KV that parses SQL is a much larger project than
the contract — while one that ignores the SQL string is a different
lib. Either choice would silently break consumers when the real
engine arrived.

**2026-06-12.** The real engine arrived (F.1); the fallback
question is moot. The "separate `pond/kv/` lib" sketch this entry
proposed remains a reasonable future lib on its own merits, but
nothing here depends on it.

---

## F.4 — `duplicate-suspected: tab-separated-row-helpers` (advisory, open)

**Tag:** `duplicate-suspected`
**Severity:** advisory.

**Description.** The unstubbed driver needs (1) "collect the
current row's columns into a tab-separated `Row.data` string" and
(2) "append a row to an accumulating `Rows.csv`". Both are equally
useful to `pond/jobs/` and `pond/migrations/` table walks, and
`pond/pq/` hand-rolls the same tab/newline accumulation in its
DataRow handler.

**2026-06-12 status.** (1) materialized as the private free fn
`__sq_collect_row(stmt)` in `db.hl`; (2) stayed two lines of glue
inline in `query_all`. Kept private — the duplication across
jobs / migrations / pq is still *suspected* rather than proven
(jobs and migrations consume `query_all`'s already-joined `Rows`,
not raw statements). Revisit if a second consumer needs
statement-level row collection: the options remain a
`pond/sqlite/rows.hl` export or a `pond/text/tabular/` promotion.

---

## F.5 — `codegen-v0-unit-fallible-unlowered` — **CLOSED 2026-06-12**

**Tag:** `codegen-v0-unit-fallible-unlowered`
**Severity:** was a deviation (three signatures off-contract).

**History.** Codegen v0 couldn't lower `() fallible(E)`, so
`bind_text` / `bind_int` / `finalize` shipped as non-fallible
`-> Int` status-code methods (0 = SQLITE_OK). The lowering was
fixed upstream by `6beb1be` (FUv0.8.2 #6); the source flip waited
for the F.1 pass.

**Resolution (2026-06-12).** All three are now
`() fallible(DbError)` member fns per the contract. Evidence: the
kv-demo's parameterized path drives `conn.bind_text(stmt, 1,
"farewell") or self.handle_unit(err);` …
`conn.finalize(stmt) or self.handle_unit(err);` and prints the
bound row's value (`prepared step: goodbye, stub era`).

(Adjacent codegen note, found while implementing: the
*non*-fallible explicit-unit shape `fn f(...) -> ()` on a member
fn still hits `codegen error: tuple type must have at least 2
elements; got 0` at HEAD 43300e5 — write such methods with the
return type omitted. Only the `() fallible(E)` form was fixed by
`6beb1be`. Doesn't affect this lib's contract surface; logged for
the next author.)

---

## F.7 — `dbdriver-interface-mismatch` (advisory, contract-level)

**Tag:** `dbdriver-interface-mismatch`
**Severity:** advisory — affects `pond/migrations` (and any
`db::DbDriver` consumer wanting a sqlite backend), not this lib's
own surface.

**Description (2026-06-12).** CONTRACTS.md § `pond/migrations`
annotates `Migrator.params.driver: db::DbDriver` with "injected:
pq::PgConn / pq::PgPool / **sqlite::Db**", and the § `pond/sqlite`
status blockquote mentions migrating onto "the `db::DbDriver`
method set". But the **locked § pond/sqlite surface** (implemented
here) is the fallible-method shape: `exec(sql) -> ExecResult
fallible(DbError)` with sqlite-local result types. `db::DbDriver`
requires the *same method names* with **non-fallible**, `ok`/`err`-
packed `db::*` result types, plus methods this contract doesn't
have (`backend` / `open` / `close` / `exec_params` /
`query_params` / `begin` / `commit` / `rollback` / `tx_status`).
One locus cannot carry both `exec` signatures, so `sqlite::Db`
does **not** structurally satisfy `db::DbDriver` as built.

**Proposed resolution.** Either (a) a thin adapter locus (in a
follow-up pass — e.g. `SqliteDriver` wrapping a `Db` and packing
DbError into the `ok`/`err` shapes, the same protocol-translation
job `pq::PgConn` does internally), or (b) amend the § migrations
comment to drop `sqlite::Db` from the injectable list until (a)
exists. Root-doc change either way — out of scope for this lib's
source pass; flagged upward.

**RESOLVED 2026-07-06 — option (a), and the adapter now lives in
THIS seed.** Built 2026-06-12 as `migs::SqliteDriver` inside
pond/migrations (the consumer that needed it first); promoted here
as `sqlite::Driver` (`driver.hl`) with its test
(`tests/driver_test.hl`). The adapter is additive to this seed's
locked surface — `Db` itself is untouched and still does NOT
satisfy `db::DbDriver`. See § pond/migrations item 11 for the
consumer-side history.

---

