 pond/tracing
======================================================================

# pond/tracing — FRICTION

Gaps, suspicions, and deviations surfaced while building this lib.

## design-question: can the runtime auto-inject spans on locus birth / dissolve?

The recursive-locus principle says spans should nest with locus
instantiation: a child locus's first span has the parent locus's
current span as its parent. v1 has the user thread the parent
`SpanId` through every `start_span(name, parent)` call — fine
for short call paths, awkward for deep towers.

What runtime auto-injection would need (sketched, not built):

1. **Locus-tower-traversal hook in the lifecycle dispatcher.**
   The runtime already walks `birth → run → drain → dissolve`
   per locus per `spec/runtime.md § Lifecycle`. Adding two
   callbacks — `on_birth(locus_id, parent_locus_id)` and
   `on_dissolve(locus_id)` — exposed to a pluggable
   `TracingHook` interface would let the Tracer subscribe
   without touching the language surface. The Tracer would
   maintain a `locus_id → SpanId` map and synthesize one span
   per locus lifetime.
2. **Locus-local "current span" reading.** Inside a member fn,
   reaching `self.__current_span` (the span the runtime opened
   at this locus's birth) so user-written `start_span(...)`
   calls can default `parent` to "the enclosing locus's span"
   when omitted. Today the user has to capture the SpanId in a
   field and thread it; with a synthetic `__current_span`
   accessor, child loci would inherit automatically.
3. **A `tracing { enabled: Bool }` capacity-slot-ish locus
   declaration.** Per-locus opt-in so the runtime isn't paying
   the span-emit cost on every locus instantiation. The Design
   already has locus-level discipline markers (`: projection ...`,
   and the `placement { }` deployment seam, F.31); a per-locus
   `: traced` opt-in would slot in alongside.
4. **A subject-binding `tracer:` field on the runtime root.**
   Today the Tracer is instantiated in `main` like any other
   locus. With auto-injection, the runtime would need a stable
   handle to the active Tracer (or accept that there can be N
   tracers, each subscribing to the same `on_birth` hook). The
   "one global Tracer" assumption is convenient but reductive;
   the Design's bus shape suggests multiple tracers should be
   the natural form, each filtering on subject.

v1 keeps the explicit-`parent` discipline. None of the above
hooks ship. The contract surface is built around explicit
threading so the auto-injection arc, when it lands, can be a
strict extension (default `parent` to current-span, not
require it).

## blocked-on-compiler-gap: topic decl + cross-file publish mangling — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

**2026-06-12 close.** Upstream WS3.3 (post-audit pass 2026-06-11,
HEAD `43300e5`) fixed cross-file topic resolution: `bus { publish
T; }` / `T <- v` now resolve a `topic T` declared in a sibling
`.hl` file of the publisher, intra-seed and through an imported
lib. Evidence: `hale build tracing/examples/trace-tree/` builds
and the demo's cross-seed subscriber (`subscribe
trace::SpanCompleted`) receives both spans (`trace-tree: OK`,
exit 0). A sibling `topics.hl` layout was trialed and works
under build/run, but the decl is kept CO-LOCATED in `tracer.hl`
for now — see the divergence note below.

**Residual upstream divergence (filed for the hale repo, not a
pond blocker):** `hale check <seed>` still resolves topics
file-locally and rejects the cross-file layout (`publish
references unknown topic \`SpanCompleted\``) — even on a minimal
two-file single-dir seed that `hale build` and `hale run` accept
and execute correctly. Same check-vs-build divergence family as
the WS2 test-fidelity finding in
`hale/notes/post-audit-downstream-actions-2026-06-11.md`.
Because pond's per-lib verification gate is `hale check <lib>/`
(libs have no `fn main()` to build at HEAD), the topic decl
stays co-located with its publisher until `hale check` catches
up — co-location is still legal style and keeps the gate green.
When the divergence closes upstream, restore the sibling
`topics.hl` layout mechanically.

**Prior shape.** The file-stem prefix differed between the topic
decl's mangled name (`__lib_trace_topics_SpanCompleted`) and the
publish-site's mangled name (`__lib_trace_tracer_SpanCompleted`),
forcing topics + locus into a single file (`tracer.hl`).

## deviation: export_otlp fallibility (two-channel rule) — [CLOSED 2026-06-08]

**2026-06-08 close.** Migrated to the v0.8.1 two-channel rule (#24
v0.2, commits `d565d6f` + `98910b9`). `Tracer.export_otlp` now
carries `() fallible(TraceError)` directly. Per CONTRACTS.md's
status note the payload is a `TraceError { kind; detail; }` type
declared in this seed (`types.hl`) rather than `IoError` — when
the transport stub (next entry) flips on, the underlying
`http`/`IoError` is translated via `... or fail TraceError { kind:
"io", ... }`. The workaround state (`last_error_kind` /
`last_error_detail` / `last_export_body` params) and its accessors
(`last_error_kind_str()` / `last_error_detail_str()` /
`last_export_body_str()`) are deleted. `start_span` / `end_span` /
`add_attr` stay non-fallible per contract. Seed type-checks clean
(`ok: 2 file(s) typechecked`). The example has no `export_otlp`
call site, so no consumer fix was needed. Closed.

**Prior shape (pre-v0.8.1).** Under the old blanket rule the lib
returned `()` and surfaced failure via `last_error_kind_str()` /
`last_error_detail_str()`; CONTRACTS.md declared
`export_otlp(endpoint: String) -> () fallible(IoError)`.

## deviation: export_otlp doesn't actually POST (transitive import gap) — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

**2026-06-12 close.** Upstream WS3.3 (post-audit pass 2026-06-11,
HEAD `43300e5`): the resolver now follows imports inside imported
seeds, so `tracer.hl` carries `import "../http/client" as http;`
and `export_otlp` really POSTs the OTLP batch
(`http::post(endpoint, std::bytes::from_string(body),
"application/json")`). HttpError translates to `TraceError {
kind: "io" }` via the `__raise_transport(err, endpoint) or raise`
chain (same idiom as http/client's `__raise_connect`); a non-2xx
collector answer fails with `kind: "non_2xx"`. `completed_buf`
clears ONLY on 2xx, so a failed export's batch is re-sent on
retry.

Evidence (probe app importing both libs against a local TCP
capture server, 2026-06-12): POST `/v1/traces` captured with
`Content-Type: application/json` and a body that
`python3 -m json.tool` accepts; export returns success and a
follow-up export is a buffer-empty no-op; against a dead
endpoint the caller's `or handler(err)` receives
`kind=io detail=OTLP POST to http://127.0.0.1:1/v1/traces
failed: connect_failed (...)`.

**Bug found by the unblock:** `__build_otlp_json` used
`std::json::Builder.field()` (raw wire bytes) for string values,
emitting invalid JSON (`"name": child.op`) — invisible while the
body was never sent. Flipped to `string_field()` (quoted +
escaped); ints stay `int_field()`.

**Vendoring rule (recorded in README.md):** consumers of
`export_otlp` must vendor `pond/http` alongside `pond/tracing`
so the relative import resolves in their tree (pond design rule
4; jobs→sqlite precedent). They do NOT need their own `import`
line unless they name `http::*` types themselves (the re-export
barrier is about naming, not resolution).

The option-3 sketch from the original entry (an `OtlpExporter`
sibling locus subscribing to `trace.span.completed`, removing the
transport from the Tracer) remains a valid v1.x design direction
but is no longer needed as a workaround.

## duplicate-suspected: __row_field in trace-tree example — [CLOSED, stale by the rowbuf lift]

**2026-06-12 status refresh.** Stale: the rowbuf lift already
happened. Both `tracer.hl` and the example import
`pond/_util/rowbuf` and call `rb::RowBuf { }.nth_field(...)`;
neither carries a private re-derivation anymore. Closed.

## design-question: silent no-op on double end_span / unknown id

`end_span(id)` is silent if the id isn't found in the open
buffer — same shape `add_attr(id, k, v)` uses. The reasoning:
mis-paired calls are user error, but turning them into a hard
structural failure (closure violation that drains the host
locus) is heavy for a tracing-instrumentation primitive that
should never take down its host. The empty-buffer-no-op
convention is consistent with `pond/math/stats`'s "empty input
returns 0.0" choice.

A future closure-test would assert the open-buffer is empty at
locus dissolve (every started span got ended). That's the right
shape for closure violation; not built at v1.

## design-question: completed_buf grows unbounded between exports

**2026-06-12 status refresh.** Narrowed but still open: now that
the transport is live, a successful `export_otlp` (2xx) clears
`completed_buf`, so an app on a periodic export cadence is
bounded. The unbounded cases that remain: an app that never calls
`export_otlp`, and a dead/rejecting collector (the buffer is
intentionally retained across failed exports for retry).

`export_otlp` is the only thing that clears `completed_buf`. A
long-lived Tracer in an app that never calls
`export_otlp` will accumulate forever. That's the same shape
`pond/metrics/Registry` lives with for its accumulating counters,
and the same shape pure-bus-publish loses (the SpanCompleted
topic publication is the consumer's responsibility to drain).

Possible disciplines if a workload pulls on this:
1. A `max_completed_spans` param that drops oldest on overflow.
2. Tracer.flush() that clears the buffer without exporting.
3. A separate "the Tracer is the bus publisher; the exporter is a
   subscriber; the buffer lives on the exporter not the Tracer"
   refactor — same shape "deviation: export_otlp doesn't actually
   POST" sketches.

v1 keeps the simplest shape; the friction-fix is the v1.x
refactor toward (3).

## suspicion: span counts and trace ids

**2026-06-12 status refresh.** Still open, and slightly more
live now that `export_otlp` really delivers: a strict OTLP
collector will also notice the simplified body shape
(`__build_otlp_json` emits a flattened `{service, spans[]}`
rather than nested `resourceSpans/scopeSpans`, and decimal-string
ids rather than hex trace/span ids). Permissive/debug collectors
ingest it; protocol-perfect OTLP remains the v1.x followup
alongside the `trace_id` split below.

CONTRACTS.md's `SpanId { id: String; }` carries a single id —
no `trace_id` separate from `span_id`. OTLP / W3C-tracecontext
distinguish "trace" (the whole request) from "span" (one
operation within it). v1 collapses both: every span is its own
"trace" because there's no per-request grouping. For the
demo-tree shape that's fine; for cross-service propagation it
isn't.

When the contract grows a `trace_id` (probably alongside the
`OtlpExporter` extraction above), the natural shape is:

```hale
type SpanId  { id: String; trace_id: String; }
type Span    { ... trace_id: String; }
```

Plus a `Tracer.start_root_span(name) -> SpanId` that mints a
fresh trace_id, and `Tracer.start_span(name, parent)` that
inherits parent.trace_id. Logged here for the v1.x cleanup.


## bug found by unit tests: add_attr's tab-delimited attrs mangled by row storage — OPEN 2026-07-04

`types.hl` documents `Span.attrs` as `key=value\t` entries with
a trailing tab — but spans are stored as tab-separated RowBuf
rows, so every field re-parse truncates `attrs` at its first
embedded tab: the published span loses the trailing tab, and a
SECOND `add_attr` silently loses the separator entirely
(`"db.statement=SELECT 1rows=3"`). Structural conflict: a
tab-bearing field inside a tab-delimited row. Found by
`tests/tracer_test.hl` (which pins the stable single-attr shape
and documents the mangling in a NOTE).

Fix requires a wire-shape decision, not a drive-by: either (a)
re-delimit attrs with a non-tab separator (US 0x1F or ";") —
touches the documented Span shape and any out-of-tree
subscriber reading attrs; or (b) move span storage off
tab-rows onto `bounded[Span; N]` now that bounded exists (the
tracing buffers are already bounded-by-discipline per the
completed_buf entry above — this would also close that one).
(b) is the better cut; deferred to its own pass since it
reshapes both buffers. Until then: one attr per span is the
reliable shape; multi-attr is lossy.

