# pond/tui — friction log

## glue-duplicated-from-term — RESOLVED by the stdlib move (hale #108–#110, 2026-06-10)

`glue.c` is a copy of `pond/term/glue.c` under `tui_`-prefixed
symbols, and the SGR/escape emission in `screen.hl` re-derives
what `pond/term`'s ansi.hl/style.hl provide. Two independent
blockers:

1. **G34 two-hop break** — `import "../term" as term;` would
   typecheck but every `term::Style { ... }` / `term::RawMode
   { }` literal inside this lib fails codegen (`qualified-name
   struct literal in expression position`).
2. **@ffi symbol collision** — @ffi symbols are not mangled
   (spec/ffi.md), and each imported lib's `[ffi] csrc` compiles
   into the same link. Sharing `term_*` symbols would
   duplicate-symbol any app vendoring both libs, so the copy
   *must* rename even if G34 lifted today.

**Resolved (2026-06-10):** the "or better" branch happened —
hale #108–#110 shipped the `std::term` primitives and the glue
disappeared from both libs (`glue.c` / `hale.toml` / `ffi.hl`
deleted here; event.hl reads via `std::io::stdin::read_byte`,
the renderer writes via `std::io::stdout::write_bytes`, Program
holds a `std::term::RawMode` guard field and polls
`std::term::size()`). Residual duplication vs pond/term: a few
escape-string fragments in the renderer.

**G34 closed 2026-06-12 (upstream WS3.4, 2026-06-11); collapse
evaluated, DECLINED.** Both old blockers are gone — a /tmp probe
at upstream 43300e5 confirmed `import pond/term` from an
intermediate lib with `term::Styler { profile: 3 }` /
`term::Style { bold: true }` literals in expression position
builds and runs through a two-hop chain. The collapse was still
declined because the import is heavier than the residual
duplication:

1. **What's actually duplicated** (measured by diffing screen.hl
   + program.hl against term's ansi.hl/style.hl): `rgb()` (one
   line, identical), `__color_sgr` (~10 lines, term's
   `__color_seq_at` specialized to profile 3), and ~14 short
   escape literals (DEC 2026 sync, alt screen, cursor
   show/hide/move, paste/mouse modes, SGR reset + the six attr
   fragments). All are frozen ANSI/DEC byte sequences — zero
   drift risk, no logic.
2. **No-transitive-deps rule (design rule 4):** importing term
   would force every tui consumer to vendor pond/term too. The
   "self-contained seed" property is consumer-facing value and
   is what CONTRACTS.md documents for this lib.
3. **Hot-path mismatch:** `Screen.emit_sgr` appends fragments
   straight into the reusable frame BytesBuilder per dirty cell,
   keyed off the Int attrs bitmask. term's surface
   (`Styler.prefix` / `style_prefix`) takes a `Style` record of
   Bools and returns a freshly concatenated String — collapsing
   the renderer onto it means a bitmask→record conversion plus a
   String allocation per dirty cell in the lib's hottest loop,
   or growing term a builder-append surface (a CONTRACTS surface
   change). term's profile-downgrade machinery is also dead
   weight here: the cell renderer documents direct truecolor
   emission.

Rendering byte output is unchanged (no source edits). Revisit
only if term grows a builder-oriented SGR surface in CONTRACTS
or the duplicated fragments start accumulating logic.

## no-append-str-on-bytesbuilder — RESOLVED upstream (hale #105, 2026-06-09)

**Was:** no `append_str(s: String)` on BytesBuilder; the frame
renderer byte-walked Strings through `append_u8` (~10k C calls
per full redraw).

**Fix (upstream):** `BytesBuilder.append_str(s: String)` shipped
(strlen + memcpy primitive; realloc-NULL routes through
`violate alloc_failed` like `append`). `screen.hl` now calls it
directly; the byte-walk helper is deleted. String only, not
StringView — a view carries no terminating NUL, so strlen would
overrun it (slices like `s[lo..hi]` are owned Strings and work).

## frame-clone-per-flush — CLOSED as correct-by-design (hale #105)

**Was:** `flush()` clones the frame
(`std::str::clone(self.frame.text_view())`) before the @ffi
write, pending an answer on StringView → String coercion at
@ffi boundaries.

**Answer (upstream, documented in spec/ffi.md):** StringView
does NOT coerce to a String @ffi param (no NUL at a view's
end), so the clone was correct while the write was FFI.

**Fully closed (2026-06-10):** the write is now the stdlib
path-call `std::io::stdout::write_bytes`, and StringView DOES
coerce to String at stdlib READ positions — the builder
maintains `buf[len] == 0`, so the view's recomputed pointer is
a valid C string, and the epoch guard validates freshness at
the coercion site. `flush()` passes `self.frame.text_view()`
straight through: zero-copy frame writes, no clone at all.

## unicode-width-heuristic — RESOLVED (2026-06-09, generated table)

**Was:** `char_width(cp)` was ~16 hand-picked range checks;
variation selectors, zero-width spaces, Hangul medial jamo, and
rarer wide blocks were wrong — a mis-measured glyph shifts the
rest of its row.

**Now:** `width.hl` is GENERATED by `tools/gen_width_table.py`
from Python's bundled Unicode database (unidata 15.0.0) — 480
merged non-narrow ranges as a binary-search if-tree (depth ~9),
wcwidth-compatible rules (Mn/Me/Cf zero with the U+00AD
exception, Jamo V/T zero, EAW W/F + emoji-presentation wide).
`char_width` keeps the cp==0 / ASCII fast paths and delegates
the rest. `examples/widthcheck/` pins 23 assertions across
every class; re-run it after regenerating.

**Still out of scope (grapheme-level, not codepoint-level):**
ZWJ *sequences* (👩‍🚀 measures as the sum of its scalars, not
one cluster) and regional-indicator flag pairs. Correct
handling needs grapheme segmentation, which is a text-shaping
concern beyond a cell renderer's per-scalar contract — same
line mainstream terminal emulators draw.

## limitation: textinput-no-mid-line-cursor

`TextInput` edits at end-of-line only (append + UTF-8-aware
backspace). Mid-line cursor movement / insertion needs
byte↔column mapping kept as widget state; deferred until a
consumer needs it.

**Status (2026-06-12):** still deferred by choice — no consumer
has needed it (logview's filter prompt and the keys demo are
append/backspace-only). Not an upstream gap; purely widget work
when a consumer shows up.

## paste-terminator-assumed-wellformed — RESOLVED (2026-06-09)

`__parse_paste` now strict-matches the `ESC [ 2 0 1 ~`
terminator byte-by-byte (`__paste_terminator`): an ESC inside
the payload that doesn't complete the terminator is preserved
verbatim (ESC + matched prefix appended to the payload) and
scanning continues, so pasted text containing raw escape
sequences survives intact. Verified by driving the keys demo
with a paste containing `\x1b[31m` mid-payload — reported
byte count includes the embedded escape.

## upstream: stdin-not-parkable

The frame loop poll-sleeps in
`std::io::stdin::read_byte(frame_budget)` (formerly the FFI
`tui_read_byte`, retired with the glue) —
correct and cheap (one poll(2) per frame), but a busy app
sharing the process (bus subscribers on other pools) would
prefer the input fd parked on an `where async_io` pool like
sockets are (F.35 covers recv/accept/send, not stdin).
Logged as the long-term shape; the poll loop is fine for v1.
Note the frame loop services the cooperative queue only between
frames; a TUI app that also subscribes to bus topics should keep
handlers on other pools or accept frame-granularity delivery.

**Status (2026-06-12):** still open upstream at 43300e5 —
`hale/notes/stdlib-term-primitives.md` explicitly defers
parkable stdin on `async_io` pools (F.35 covers
recv/accept/send, not stdin), and no stdin-parking surface has
shipped since. The per-frame poll remains correct and cheap.

## panic-exit-bypasses-atexit — RESOLVED upstream (hale #106, 2026-06-09)

The F.30b stale-view panic now exits via `exit(1)` instead of
`_exit(1)`, so this lib's atexit restore (termios + leave alt
screen + show cursor) runs on every runtime panic path. See
`FRICTION.log` for the full entry; upstream carries a
regression test modeled on exactly this glue pattern.

## interface-field-skips-dissolve-cascade — upstream gap + framework workaround

**What:** the F.29 lifecycle cascade dissolves `LocusRef`-typed
param fields, but an INTERFACE-typed param field (`app: App` on
`Program`) wraps its locus in a `{data, vtable}` fat pointer the
cascade does not walk — the underlying locus's `dissolve()`
never fires when the holder dissolves. Found building procpanel:
its `Panel.dissolve()` (which TERMs all supervised children) ran
on no exit path, leaking child processes on max_frames / EOF
quits while the explicit `q` path (in `update`) cleaned up fine.

**Repro shape:** `locus H { params { a: I; } }` where `I` is an
interface and the bound value is a locus with a `dissolve()`
body — instantiate H, let it dissolve, observe the inner
dissolve never runs.

**Workaround (framework-level, shipped):** `Program.run()`
delivers one final `Event { kind: "quit" }` to `app.update`
after the loop ends, on every quit path — apps do teardown
there instead of in `dissolve()`. This is arguably the better
surface anyway (Elm apps expect a last message, and it works
under any future cascade semantics), so it stays even when the
gap closes.

**Proposed upstream unblock:** extend
`emit_locus_field_dissolves` to interface-typed fields (the fat
pointer's data slot is the locus; its type is known at the
coercion site — a per-(locus, interface) dissolve thunk beside
the vtable would carry it).

**Status (2026-06-12):** re-verified still open at upstream
43300e5 — `emit_locus_field_dissolves`
(`crates/hale-codegen/src/locus/dissolve.rs`) still matches only
`CodegenTy::LocusRef` fields and `continue`s past everything
else, so interface-typed fields remain unwalked. The
final-`quit`-event workaround stays the framework contract
(and stays even if the cascade gap closes).


## unbounded-alloc triage — 2026-07-04 (deferred: needs a dedicated pass)

The default-on analysis flags 45 sites, in two classes:

1. **`Event` structs** returned per input byte from the parser
   free fns (`__key_from_byte`, `__utf8_char`) and consumed in
   `Program::run`'s app-lifetime loop — true accumulation, but
   at human input rate (~100 B/keypress; MBs over a full day of
   continuous typing).
2. **`Screen` vec-inserts** (`set_cell`/`clear`/`resize`/
   `flush`) — per-frame cell churn against the cell grid;
   needs real reading to split refill-in-place (fine) from
   growth (leak).

Neither class is fixed in the 2026-07-04 pass — 45 sites in the
hot render path deserve their own focused bounded-memory pass
with before/after RSS measurement (same bar the upstream audit
used), not drive-by edits. Interim risk assessment: interactive
apps are human-rate bounded; the logview/metricsdash demos run
minutes, not weeks. Logged as the next tui work item.

*2026-08-03 re-baseline (hale v0.13.0):* 45 → **41 sites**, and
class 2 mostly resolved itself: the v0.11.12 vec `.set` retire
fix (replaced elements go to the arena freelist; `.set` is no
longer an accumulation channel) cleared every `set_cell` /
`clear` / `flush` refill site — exactly the split the entry
above wanted "real reading" for, answered upstream. Remaining:
39 `Event` struct sites in event.hl (class 1, human-rate
bounded, unchanged) and `Screen::resize` ×2 (`screen.hl:58-59`
— genuine growth, but per-resize-event, not per-frame). The
dedicated pass is now scoped to class 1 + resize only; the
per-frame cell-churn half of the concern is gone.

