 pond/websocket
======================================================================

# pond/websocket — FRICTION

Gaps, suspicions, and deviations from RFC 6455 surfaced while
building this lib.

## upstream 9bc53d5: Stream send/recv migrated to fallible(IoError) — [CLOSED 2026-07-15]

hale `9bc53d5` (breaking, `feat(stdlib)!`) respelled the Stream
surface: `send_bytes` now succeeds with Unit and fails with
IoError (previously -1/0 sentinels); `recv`/`recv_bytes` fail
with IoError on genuine errors and return empty for BOTH EOF and
a recv-timeout expiry. server.hl carried six pre-migration call
sites — four bare `stream.send_bytes(x);` statements (handshake
400/101, read_msg pong/close echoes) and two
`let n = stream.send_bytes(...)` value bindings (pump_recv's
proactive ping; send_frame's error check).

Migrated this pass (all six sites; no public-surface change):

- Best-effort sends take `or discard` — a dead peer is detected
  by the pong deadline, not the send error, exactly as before.
- `send_frame` translates the IoError to the lib's WsError
  channel via a fallible member handler
  (`or self.__send_frame_failed(err)`) that also flips
  `connected` — the same observable behavior as the old `n < 0`
  branch, minus the short-write case that could never occur
  (send_bytes loops until the whole frame is written).
- client.hl needed nothing: it drives the raw primitives
  (`std::io::tcp::__send_bytes`, path-call `recv_into`), which
  9bc53d5 left alone.

REQUIRES hale >= 9bc53d5 — on older compilers the `or` clauses
are "or on non-fallible call" errors. Gates at 9bc53d5:
`hale check` ok, `hale test websocket/` 2/2, echo-client builds.
(Pre-migration baseline at 9bc53d5: both codegen gates fail with
"method `send_bytes` returns no value but is used in expression
position".)

NOTE for the sibling libs: `pond/pq` (5 sites) and
`pond/http` client (3 sites) have the same class of breakage and
need the same pass; pq's unmigrated sites additionally surface as
an LLVM verifier ICE instead of a typecheck error (reported
upstream in the sme-hale handoff addendum, 2026-07-15).

## stdlib gap: SHA-1 + base64 for handshake validation — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

**2026-06-12 resolution.** `std::crypto::sha1` and
`std::text::base64::encode` are in the stdlib (the server side's
`compute_accept` had already adopted them on 2026-05-28). The
client side caught up this pass:

- `build_request` now takes the key as a parameter; `WsClient`
  generates a fresh `Sec-WebSocket-Key` per connect — 16 CSPRNG
  bytes via `std::os::getrandom(16)`, base64-encoded. The RFC
  § 1.3 sample-nonce constant is gone.
- `parse_response(b, expected_accept)` validates the server's
  `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` against `compute_accept(key)` per § 4.1
  step 4 (case-insensitive header lookup via the new
  `header_value` free fn; pass `""` to skip for lower-level
  callers). Mismatch / absence fails the connect with
  `kind = "handshake"`.

Verified against a raw-socket RFC 6455 echo server: correct
accept ⇒ upgrade completes; corrupted accept ⇒
`open failed: kind=handshake detail=Sec-WebSocket-Accept
mismatch: ...`. The free-fn surface change (`build_request` /
`parse_response` arity) is reflected in README.md; CONTRACTS.md
lists those free fns by name only, so no contract delta beyond
the status note.

Original entry (for the record): v1 stdlib had neither sha1 nor
base64, so the lib shipped a deterministic key and trusted the
peer's accept header.

## stdlib gap: CSPRNG-seeded masking key — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

**2026-06-12 resolution.** Outbound mask keys are now drawn from
`std::os::getrandom(4)` **per frame** (`WsClient.next_mask_seed`),
not per connect: RFC 6455 § 5.3 requires that observing one
frame's key (they ride the wire in cleartext) must not make the
next key predictable, so the previously proposed
"reseed the LCG once per `connect_once()`" would NOT have
satisfied the RFC — any observed mask key replays the public LCG
chain. The `mask_seed` LCG is retained only as the fallback when
`getrandom` fails (never in practice; `/dev/urandom` fallback is
inside the primitive) so sends still proceed.

Verified on the wire: a 4-frame session showed 4 unrelated mask
keys (`5cf4b6e9`, `395379e3`, `a8e85167`, `ce6491e7`), vs. the
old deterministic `0xCAFEBABE`-seeded chain.

Cost note: one `getrandom(2)` syscall per outbound frame. Fine
for this lib's duty cycle; a throughput-focused v2 could buffer
entropy in a child builder.

## substrate gap: pong-deadline wall-clock tracking — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

**2026-06-12 resolution.** Wired, on both loci, using
`std::io::tcp::set_recv_timeout` (v0.8.1, commit `1ab9f71`) plus
the two pieces upstream shipped in #132 (`b716696`, "WsClient
liveness, Part A"): the handle-aware
`std::io::tls::set_recv_timeout` sibling, and the distinguishable
`-2` "timed out, retryable" sentinel on `recv_into` (both tcp and
tls) — without which a deadline expiry was indistinguishable from
a fatal recv error.

Two-phase deadline design (`WsClient`, mirrored on
`WsServerConn`):

- Steady state arms `ping_interval` (default 30s) as the recv
  deadline (`set_recv_deadline`, tls-aware on the client). Any
  inbound bytes re-arm it.
- On a `-2` expiry, the locus sends a proactive ping (best-effort,
  `or discard` / unmasked server-shape) and re-arms with
  `pong_timeout` (default 10s).
- A second consecutive expiry declares the peer dead: socket torn
  down; `WsClient.read_msg` reconnects (auto_reconnect) or returns
  false with the new `last_error.kind = "timeout"`; `WsServerConn.
  read_msg` returns false with the same kind.
- `pong_timeout` also bounds the handshake waits (client: response
  after the upgrade request — a server that accepts then goes mute
  fails the connect in `pong_timeout`, not never; server: the
  inbound upgrade request itself).
- Either cadence at `0` disables that deadline (`d = 0` disables
  SO_RCVTIMEO) — the pre-2026-06-12 fully-blocking behavior.
- `WsServerConn` converted from `Stream.recv_bytes` to
  `std::io::tcp::recv_into(conn_fd, rx_buf, ...)` in the process
  (the Bytes-returning `recv_bytes` can't distinguish the timeout
  sentinel from EOF; recv_into also drops a per-chunk copy).

`ping_interval` / `pong_timeout` thereby graduate from documentary
to enforced; `WsServerConn` gains the same two params (additive,
defaults preserve RFC-compliant clients — any conforming client
answers pings). Verified end-to-end on loopback with 1s/1s
cadences: silent-after-handshake server received the client's
ping at t+1.0s and the client unblocked at t+2.03s with
`kind=timeout` (previously: infinite hang); the mirrored
server-side run pinged its silent client at t+1.04s and dropped
it at t+2.06s.

Residual (logged, not blocking): a `set_recv_timeout` failure is
logged-and-dropped (`or discard`) — degradation is back to the
old blocking recv, and lifecycle/pump methods can't be
`fallible(E)` under the two-channel rule anyway.

## substrate gap: IPv6 literal hosts in URL parsing

**2026-06-12 retest — still open** (at hale `b716696`). The
original note misstated the mechanism: `parse_url` splits
host:port on the **first** `:` (`std::str::index_of`), which is
equivalent for IPv4 / hostnames (≤1 colon) but maps
`ws://[::1]:9107/` to host `"["` (and the port parse fails back
to the scheme default). Repro: connect attempt resolves the
garbage host — `lotus_tcp_connect: resolve [: Temporary failure
in name resolution` → `kind=io`. No clean parse-stage rejection
either.

Resolution: bracket-aware split (host = `[...]` contents, port
after `]:`), plus last-colon split for the bracketless form.
Logged as a TODO; the demo doesn't exercise IPv6 and
`std::io::tcp::connect` is AF_INET-only today anyway, so the fix
waits for an AF_INET6-capable dial upstream.

## codegen gap: Bytes default in params — [CLOSED 2026-06-12]

**2026-06-12 retest.** Both halves retired:

- `b""` now lexes and codegens as a `Bytes` param default —
  probe `params { x: Bytes = b""; }` builds and runs at hale
  `b716696` (G5 is stale at HEAD).
- The lib no longer has any `Bytes`-typed param needing it:
  `rx_buf` / `frag_buf` / `scratch_buf` became
  `std::bytes::BytesBuilder` child loci (F.29/F.30 pass,
  2026-05-20). Inline `b""` literals are already used freely
  (`types.hl` view defaults, `server.hl` control frames).

pond/subprocess and pond/http/client document the same
workaround and can drop it on their next source pass (their
FRICTION logs, not this one).

## deviation from CONTRACTS.md: no `fallible(WsError)` on locus methods — [CLOSED 2026-06-08]

**2026-06-08 resolution.** Migrated to the v0.8.1 two-channel rule
(#24 v0.2). The owner-called outbound send surface now carries
`fallible(WsError)` directly; callers address with
`or raise` / `or fail ...` / `or <substitute>` / `or discard`:

- `WsClient.send_text` / `send_bytes` / `close` →
  `() fallible(WsError)`. The internal `send_frame_internal`
  helper is also `fallible(WsError)` so the user-facing fns
  bubble it via `or raise` (and `close`'s best-effort write via
  `or discard`).
- `WsServerConn.send_text` / `send_binary` / `close` (and the
  shared `send_frame` helper) → `() fallible(WsError)`, same
  shape.

New caller pattern:

```hale
client.send_text("hello") or raise;        // bubble to enclosing fallible scope
client.send_text("hello") or discard;      // best-effort, drop the error
conn.close() or discard;
```

**Stayed non-fallible (substrate-facing surfaces).** Per the
v0.8.1 narrowing these keep the Bool-return + `self.last_error`
value-channel shape rather than `fallible(E)`:

- Lifecycle: `WsClient.birth` / `dissolve`, `WsServerConn.birth`.
- The owner-driven blocking pumps `WsClient.open` / `read_msg`
  and `WsServerConn.handshake` / `read_msg` — these report a
  rich `WsError` via `self.last_error` while returning `Bool`
  for the loop predicate (`while conn.read_msg() { ... }`), the
  recv-loop shape the contract documents. They are intentionally
  left on the value channel; flipping them would force every
  run-loop predicate through an `or` disposition.
- The structural-channel error-check fns
  (`io_substitute_int` / `handshake_fail` / `framing_fail_hdr`
  in client.hl, `__raise_framing` in server.hl) and the
  `try_peel_one` inbound control path remain — `try_peel_one`'s
  ping-reply send is addressed with `or discard` (it is not an
  owner-called send). There are no bus-subscribed handler fns in
  this lib.

`self.last_error` / the `expose last_error` contract field are
retained: still the channel for `open` / `read_msg` / handshake
failures.

## RFC 6455 conformance scope

The lib implements the client (`WsClient`) plus a per-connection
server-side wrapper (`WsServerConn`: upgrade handshake +
unmasked server-shape framing via `emit_server_frame`). Still
deliberately omitted at v1:

- Subprotocol negotiation (`Sec-WebSocket-Protocol`)
- Extensions (`Sec-WebSocket-Extensions`, including permessage-
  deflate — which would need an inflate primitive)
- Origin enforcement (server-side anti-CSRF gate)
- An accept-loop / HTTP/1.1 upgrade router (the consumer owns
  the listen/accept/dispatch shape; see `server.hl`'s head
  comment for the F.35 pattern)

## suspected duplicate: arithmetic XOR-byte helper — **CLOSED 2026-07-04**

**Closed:** upstream shipped `BytesBuilder.xor_mask(src, key)`
(hale `227db57`, "the WebSocket masking primitive") — one
reserve + word-at-a-time pass XORing against an LE-packed
repeating 4-byte key. `frame.hl` now masks (`emit_frame`) and
unmasks (`unmask_into` slow path) through it; the local
arithmetic `xor8` bit-decomposition helper and `pick_mask_byte`
are deleted (replaced by `pack_mask_key`, packing mk0..mk3
little-endian). Verified: masked + unmasked frames round-trip
byte-identically (emit → peek_header → unmask_into probe);
echo-client builds. The broader ask (bitwise XOR at the
language level) is still real for crypto-shaped code, but no
pond lib hand-rolls byte XOR anymore. Original entry below.

[historical] `pond/_util/intfloat::xor8` is the only place pond computes
byte-wise XOR (v1 has no bitwise XOR in expression position —
G12 in `pond/KNOWN_GOTCHAS.md`). This lib uses it for both
frame mask + unmask. Same shape `pond/crypto::hmac_sha256` will
need if it ever lifts the XOR-byte primitive out of its inline
implementation.

A `std::math::xor8(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int` primitive (or surfacing
bitwise XOR at the language level) would let `_util/intfloat`
collapse. Logged.

## unbounded-alloc triage: 9 → 0 warnings — 2026-07-04

The default-on unbounded-alloc analysis flagged nine sites; the
upstream full-corpus audit had already confirmed this lib's
per-message class as a true production leak ("pond websocket
last_message.kind per message"). All nine fixed:

- `last_message` (client `try_peel_one`, server `read_msg` ×2):
  whole-value `WsMessage` replace per received message →
  in-place field mutation; `kind` re-anchors only on a
  text/binary transition (steady single-kind streams allocate
  nothing per message).
- `last_error` (client `reparse_url` + the four recv-path
  bridge handlers): whole-value `WsError` replace per failed
  frame/dial → in-place `kind`/`detail` field writes.
- server `pump_recv`: the fd `Stream` wrapper, the empty ping
  frame, and the pong-death `WsLogEvent` hoisted above the
  connection-lifetime loop (all loop-invariant).

`hale check websocket` is now warning-free. Residual known
limitation (upstream, not ours): replacing a heap String FIELD
still orphans the old clone in the locus arena (the TP-3
anchor-clone class — retirement shipped for @form(hashmap)
cells only; plain-field retire is staged upstream in
notes/anchor-retirement.md). Our in-place shape minimizes the
churn to error-path detail strings and kind transitions.

## bug found by unit tests: parse_request dropped the terminal header line — FIXED 2026-07-04

The first `hale test` pass caught a real parser bug:
`handshake.hl parse_request`'s header walk hit the final line
before the terminating \r\n\r\n (which has no \r\n of its own
inside the scanned window), took the `line_end < 0` branch, and
advanced the cursor WITHOUT applying the line. `build_request`
emits `Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13` last, so a build → parse
round-trip silently lost the version. `header_value` handled
the same terminal-line case correctly — the gap was
parse_request-specific. Fixed by applying the remainder line in
the no-CRLF branch; guarded by the regression assert in
`tests/handshake_test.hl` ("terminal header line parses").
First concrete proof of the tests-as-second-codegen-gate
convention paying for itself on day one.
